systems on behalf of the user.

193
00:09:21,540 --> 00:09:24,178
So the other
application that does

194
00:09:24,178 --> 00:09:26,220
a lot of interacting
directly with other computer

195
00:09:26,220 --> 00:09:29,480
systems on the behalf of
users is background agents.

196
00:09:29,480 --> 00:09:33,540
So this is something like the
dev encoding agent Resolve's SRE

197
00:09:33,540 --> 00:09:39,140
agent, Ramps Inspect coding
agent, or Open Inspect,

198
00:09:39,140 --> 00:09:40,740
or OpenClaw.

199
00:09:40,740 --> 00:09:42,260
These are all
systems where there

200
00:09:42,260 --> 00:09:45,820
is a human relatively tight.

201
00:09:45,820 --> 00:09:48,253
They probably produce
many of the tokens

202
00:09:48,253 --> 00:09:49,920
in the prompt, the
most important tokens

203
00:09:49,920 --> 00:09:52,480
in the prompt
themselves directly,

204
00:09:52,480 --> 00:09:54,820
but they aren't necessarily
waiting for the result.

205
00:09:54,820 --> 00:09:56,580
They're in a meeting.

206
00:09:56,580 --> 00:09:58,400
Somebody asked them to
implement a feature,

207
00:09:58,400 --> 00:10:03,120
and they've tagged Devin
or Inspector or whatever

208
00:10:03,120 --> 00:10:06,080
to implement the feature
for them and open a PR.

209
00:10:06,080 --> 00:10:09,180
So you don't have interactive
human latency constraints.

210
00:10:09,180 --> 00:10:12,080
You just have some
latency constraint

211
00:10:12,080 --> 00:10:16,160
on the order of many seconds,
many minutes, many hours.

212
00:10:16,160 --> 00:10:18,800
And then finally, data
processors-- the prototypes here

213
00:10:18,800 --> 00:10:21,580
