244
00:11:42,620 --> 00:11:44,460
So you'll hear service
level agreements--

245
00:11:44,460 --> 00:11:46,060
SLAs or service
level objectives,

246
00:11:46,060 --> 00:11:51,920
SLOs, to describe the
constraints or expectations

247
00:11:51,920 --> 00:11:57,560
in terms of failure rates,
performance like speed,

248
00:11:57,560 --> 00:12:00,360
and cost as well.

249
00:12:00,360 --> 00:12:04,800
And this is the handoff point
between the engineer of a system

250
00:12:04,800 --> 00:12:11,040
and the other
stakeholders in a system.

251
00:12:11,040 --> 00:12:15,080
So we were working with a
lot of customers, who wanted

252
00:12:15,080 --> 00:12:16,620
to build LLM inference systems.

253
00:12:16,620 --> 00:12:19,910
And we would ask them-- they
wanted our help, our expertise,

254
00:12:19,910 --> 00:12:22,160
and getting information about
the workload out of them

255
00:12:22,160 --> 00:12:23,580
was challenging.

256
00:12:23,580 --> 00:12:27,080
So I developed this thing that's
available in our LLM engineers

257
00:12:27,080 --> 00:12:28,080
Almanac.

258
00:12:28,080 --> 00:12:30,920
That's a little
Madlibs that asks

259
00:12:30,920 --> 00:12:33,440
you to define your workload.

260
00:12:33,440 --> 00:12:35,760
So click each one
of these things

261
00:12:35,760 --> 00:12:37,680
and select like
which model you want

262
00:12:37,680 --> 00:12:42,220
to serve, what your
constraints on performance are,

263
00:12:42,220 --> 00:12:46,060
and both in terms of
latency in the top right,

264
00:12:46,060 --> 00:12:51,860
and in terms of throughput
on the bottom left.

265
00:12:51,860 --> 00:12:54,660
So you can check that out.

266
00:12:54,660 --> 00:12:56,480
The models in it are
a little out of date,

267
00:12:56,480 --> 00:12:59,500
but stay tuned for
a major update soon.

268
00:12:59,500 --> 00:13:01,507
That should keep
it much fresher.

269
00:13:01,507 --> 00:13:03,340
So really what I just
want to point out here

270
00:13:03,340 --> 00:13:06,740
is like this specific
selection of ways

271
00:13:06,740 --> 00:13:10,460
to define what the workload
is that you want to run.

272
00:13:10,460 --> 00:13:12,460
Because this clarified
and shortened

273
00:13:12,460 --> 00:13:15,100
a lot of otherwise
difficult conversations.

274
00:13:15,100 --> 00:13:18,620
So the key thing here is
to define these workloads

275
00:13:18,620 --> 00:13:20,520
via a bunch of metrics.

276
00:13:20,520 --> 00:13:24,140
So you have queries
per second, QPS.

277
00:13:24,140 --> 00:13:25,840
This one is under
control of users.

278
00:13:25,840 --> 00:13:27,108
It's often tricky to forecast.

279
00:13:27,108 --> 00:13:28,900
You make something you
don't whether people

280
00:13:28,900 --> 00:13:32,500
are going to want it or use
it, and there's quite a bit

281
00:13:32,500 --> 00:13:34,620
of variability or seasonality.

282
00:13:34,620 --> 00:13:37,840
People often don't think about
that variability or seasonality.

283
00:13:37,840 --> 00:13:41,020
But if you can try and
figure that out early.

284
00:13:41,020 --> 00:13:43,620
That's important because that
changes how difficult it is.

285
00:13:43,620 --> 00:13:46,080
The higher the ratio
between your average traffic

286
00:13:46,080 --> 00:13:47,920
and your peak traffic,
the more difficult

287
00:13:47,920 --> 00:13:50,000
it is to serve a system.

288
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:52,320
So that's something
to get ahead of early.

289
00:13:52,320 --> 00:13:54,520
Within those queries--
those queries usually

290
00:13:54,520 --> 00:13:56,240
that might be user
queries, or it

291
00:13:56,240 --> 00:13:59,400
might be queries generated
by the model via tool calls.

292
00:13:59,400 --> 00:14:01,280
What you want to find
out about those queries

293
00:14:01,280 --> 00:14:04,860
is how many tokens you can
expect in those queries--

294
00:14:04,860 --> 00:14:07,460
how many tokens in the
inputs during profile phases,

295
00:14:07,460 --> 00:14:10,340
and how many tokens in the
output in DECOD phases.

296
00:14:10,340 --> 00:14:12,815
So this one's really annoying
because it's not only

297
00:14:12,815 --> 00:14:13,940
under control of the users.

298
00:14:13,940 --> 00:14:15,440
It's also under
control of the model

299
00:14:15,440 --> 00:14:18,600
because the model decides when
to emit the end of text token.

300
00:14:18,600 --> 00:14:20,920
And so your output
lengths are actually not

301
00:14:20,920 --> 00:14:23,300
necessarily deterministic.

302
00:14:23,300 --> 00:14:28,740
Or you can try and make them
deterministic in some settings,

303
00:14:28,740 --> 00:14:30,880
but even then, they
may be literally

304
00:14:30,880 --> 00:14:33,620
deterministic on the same
inputs and same seed,

305
00:14:33,620 --> 00:14:36,940
but they're very hard to reason
about because they're coming out

306
00:14:36,940 --> 00:14:38,860
of the statistical model.

307
00:14:38,860 --> 00:14:40,660
So that's bad news.

308
00:14:40,660 --> 00:14:42,980
It's a little bit
different-- it's maybe

309
00:14:42,980 --> 00:14:45,740
similar to a database in that
when the data in the database

310
00:14:45,740 --> 00:14:47,520
changes, query
performance changes.

311
00:14:47,520 --> 00:14:50,140
And so you just have to
be empirical about it

312
00:14:50,140 --> 00:14:51,740
in many cases.

313
00:14:51,740 --> 00:14:53,240
So you can estimate this.

314
00:14:53,240 --> 00:14:54,940
This is maybe a
little bit easier

315
00:14:54,940 --> 00:14:56,760
than estimating how many
queries there are going to be,

316
00:14:56,760 --> 00:14:58,343
because that depends
on whether you're

317
00:14:58,343 --> 00:15:02,060
on the front page of Hacker News
or tweeted out by an influencer

318
00:15:02,060 --> 00:15:03,620
or whatever.

319
00:15:03,620 --> 00:15:07,260
That determines your
queries per second.

320
00:15:07,260 --> 00:15:09,080
Prefix reuse-- this
is another one.

321
00:15:09,080 --> 00:15:12,320
How often when a sequence of
tokens comes in from a user

322
00:15:12,320 --> 00:15:14,600
is it some tokens that
you've seen before?

323
00:15:14,600 --> 00:15:17,060
This is really
important because you

324
00:15:17,060 --> 00:15:21,000
can cache your previous
computations and store them.

325
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:24,180
People get very elaborate
with these approaches.

326
00:15:24,180 --> 00:15:27,140
This is pretty powerful,
especially in situations

327
00:15:27,140 --> 00:15:30,700
with lower interactivity,
because you can just

328
00:15:30,700 --> 00:15:34,200
delay, turn, what would
be GPU computation

329
00:15:34,200 --> 00:15:36,400
into loads from storage.

330
00:15:36,400 --> 00:15:43,360
And that's really
nice for your costs

331
00:15:43,360 --> 00:15:47,080
at often at a cost to latency.

332
00:15:47,080 --> 00:15:51,960
So I mostly think of it as
something that I use when--

333
00:15:51,960 --> 00:15:53,520
aggressive kV caching
in a case where

334
00:15:53,520 --> 00:15:57,710
I have users with a
greater latency tolerance.

335
00:15:57,710 --> 00:15:59,460
This is mostly under
control of the users,

336
00:15:59,460 --> 00:16:04,320
but given that users write short
prompts of dozens of tokens

337
00:16:04,320 --> 00:16:08,060
and models observe prompts
with thousands of tokens,

338
00:16:08,060 --> 00:16:10,620
this is in fact under
the control of something.

339
00:16:10,620 --> 00:16:12,720
Maybe a harness or
other software system

340
00:16:12,720 --> 00:16:15,200
that you may have
some control over.

341
00:16:15,200 --> 00:16:18,600
And so then you can be
smart or dumb about it,

342
00:16:18,600 --> 00:16:22,400
and you can also
ban people using

343
00:16:22,400 --> 00:16:25,680
unhinged lobster-themed
software from your endpoints

344
00:16:25,680 --> 00:16:28,100
if it busts your cache.

345
00:16:28,100 --> 00:16:28,600
Yeah.

346
00:16:28,600 --> 00:16:33,680
So then I've alluded to latency
budgets a couple of times.

347
00:16:33,680 --> 00:16:37,260
There's basically two that
matter-- the time to first token

348
00:16:37,260 --> 00:16:41,620
and the time per output
token or inter-token latency.

349
00:16:41,620 --> 00:16:43,080
So time to first token.

350
00:16:43,080 --> 00:16:46,060
How long does it take to get the
first token or first byte back

351
00:16:46,060 --> 00:16:47,260
to the user?

352
00:16:47,260 --> 00:16:50,020
And then how long does it take
to produce each sequential token

353
00:16:50,020 --> 00:16:51,540
after that?

354
00:16:51,540 --> 00:16:54,900
Time to last token also
matters, but because it

355
00:16:54,900 --> 00:16:59,540
depends on the output length
and very, very directly, very

356
00:16:59,540 --> 00:17:02,560
painfully and obviously
depends on that output length,

357
00:17:02,560 --> 00:17:05,859
it's often easier to reason
about time per output token

358
00:17:05,859 --> 00:17:07,800
and output lengths separately.

359
00:17:07,800 --> 00:17:10,520
And then when you need to
think about time to last token,

360
00:17:10,520 --> 00:17:11,772
you combine them together.

361
00:17:11,772 --> 00:17:13,480
And then once you
start doing tool calls,

362
00:17:13,480 --> 00:17:14,920
all hell breaks loose.

363
00:17:14,920 --> 00:17:19,938
And you have to go back to being
pretty empirical with live data.

364
00:17:19,938 --> 00:17:22,480
So latency budget is going to
be defined by your application.

365
00:17:22,480 --> 00:17:25,147
It's one of the main things that
separated the three application

366
00:17:25,147 --> 00:17:27,900
classes we discussed
on the previous slide.

367
00:17:27,900 --> 00:17:30,920
And maybe one important
thing about this

368
00:17:30,920 --> 00:17:33,260
is with time per output token
and input token latency,

369
00:17:33,260 --> 00:17:35,720
you really want to
be thinking per user.

370
00:17:35,720 --> 00:17:37,720
What is the experience
of an individual user

371
00:17:37,720 --> 00:17:40,060
or an individual
request in this system?

372
00:17:40,060 --> 00:17:44,600
In every case, except the bulk
data processing because that

373
00:17:44,600 --> 00:17:45,480
is what--

374
00:17:45,480 --> 00:17:47,380
these are defined
by the application.

375
00:17:47,380 --> 00:17:49,140
They're defined by
the user experience.

376
00:17:49,140 --> 00:17:51,620
And so that's what you
need to care about,

377
00:17:51,620 --> 00:17:53,920
not the machine experience.

378
00:17:53,920 --> 00:17:55,000
Yeah.

379
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:57,720
One last tip about
this is you really

380
00:17:57,720 --> 00:17:59,700
want to reason about
this per replica.

381
00:17:59,700 --> 00:18:03,020
So you have a whole
system-- particular QPS.

382
00:18:03,020 --> 00:18:05,120
QPS is something people
will say like, oh, I

383
00:18:05,120 --> 00:18:07,720
think I'm going to have a
thousand concurrent users,

384
00:18:07,720 --> 00:18:10,020
and they'll send a query
every couple of seconds,

385
00:18:10,020 --> 00:18:14,320
and then they'll reason
their way to total QPS.

386
00:18:14,320 --> 00:18:15,880
That is very helpful to know.

387
00:18:15,880 --> 00:18:17,840
That gives you the
aggregate demand,

388
00:18:17,840 --> 00:18:19,440
but you're going
to probably end up

389
00:18:19,440 --> 00:18:22,680
serving this with multiple
replicas, multiple machines

390
00:18:22,680 --> 00:18:25,080
running an inference engine.

391
00:18:25,080 --> 00:18:28,100
And so you need to be able to--

392
00:18:28,100 --> 00:18:30,515
and that's the amount--

393
00:18:30,515 --> 00:18:32,140
there's a certain
number of queries you

394
00:18:32,140 --> 00:18:33,740
could fit in a single replica.

395
00:18:33,740 --> 00:18:36,540
And then like hitting
these latency budgets,

396
00:18:36,540 --> 00:18:37,340
and then you can--

397
00:18:37,340 --> 00:18:40,220
and also your prefix reuse.

398
00:18:40,220 --> 00:18:44,420
And then so that sets
the number of queries

399
00:18:44,420 --> 00:18:45,680
you can handle per replica.

400
00:18:45,680 --> 00:18:50,020
And then you add replicas, until
you can satisfy the total load.

401
00:18:50,020 --> 00:18:52,300
So this is different
than a database,

402
00:18:52,300 --> 00:18:56,140
where you try to avoid
having multiple replicas,

403
00:18:56,140 --> 00:18:58,100
until your IPO--

404
00:18:58,100 --> 00:19:03,280
and because inference is
independent across the engines.

405
00:19:03,280 --> 00:19:06,060
Each of them can operate
essentially independently apart

406
00:19:06,060 --> 00:19:10,100
from the prefix caching.

407
00:19:10,100 --> 00:19:15,580
So for our three
archetypes, the figure

408
00:19:15,580 --> 00:19:18,960
of merit that you care about
in each workload is different.

409
00:19:18,960 --> 00:19:21,140
So for chatbot plus, you
care about interactivity,

410
00:19:21,140 --> 00:19:23,360
which is tokens per
second per user--

411
00:19:23,360 --> 00:19:26,600
mostly output tokens
per second per user.

412
00:19:26,600 --> 00:19:29,320
For a background agent, you
care about time to last token

413
00:19:29,320 --> 00:19:31,880
because that's when you produce
the result that the human is

414
00:19:31,880 --> 00:19:40,440
waiting on-- the PR, the design
doc, the generated image.

415
00:19:40,440 --> 00:19:43,120
With data processors,
you care mostly

416
00:19:43,120 --> 00:19:46,880
about how quickly you can
chunk through stuff, so

417
00:19:46,880 --> 00:19:48,580
like mega tokens per dollar.

418
00:19:48,580 --> 00:19:51,160

419
00:19:51,160 --> 00:19:51,760
Great.

420
00:19:51,760 --> 00:19:54,000
And that comes from your
throughput in tokens

421
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:56,160
per second aggregate,
appropriately mixed

422
00:19:56,160 --> 00:19:58,480
between input and output.

423
00:19:58,480 --> 00:20:01,280
So I said you want to reason
about at the level of one

424
00:20:01,280 --> 00:20:02,380
replica then scale up.

425
00:20:02,380 --> 00:20:04,480
So how do you
benchmark one replica?

426
00:20:04,480 --> 00:20:06,080
Quick and easy recipe.

427
00:20:06,080 --> 00:20:08,258
First, figure out
your minimum latency.

428
00:20:08,258 --> 00:20:10,800
What's the fastest you could
run this thing if every user got

429
00:20:10,800 --> 00:20:11,900
their own replica?

430
00:20:11,900 --> 00:20:14,080
So run n queries one
at a time, and then

431
00:20:14,080 --> 00:20:18,420
record one over the mean
latency max throughput.

432
00:20:18,420 --> 00:20:22,600
Dump n queries at once, and then
record n over the total latency.

433
00:20:22,600 --> 00:20:26,460
So this is if all
your requests hit

434
00:20:26,460 --> 00:20:29,120
the replica at the
same time, wait,

435
00:20:29,120 --> 00:20:30,880
see how quickly it can
chunk through them.

436
00:20:30,880 --> 00:20:32,900
Expose the maximum
amount of parallelism

437
00:20:32,900 --> 00:20:35,060
to the engine and the hardware.

438
00:20:35,060 --> 00:20:40,260
So that will give you a minimum
throughput and minimum latency

439
00:20:40,260 --> 00:20:44,320
and maximum throughput with
a generally higher latency.

440
00:20:44,320 --> 00:20:47,060
And then you can measure the
behavior of replicas in between

441
00:20:47,060 --> 00:20:49,840
want to sweep over
in between rates.

442
00:20:49,840 --> 00:20:52,000
So this badly needs a diagram.

443
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:53,562
So the idea is here
I send one query.

444
00:20:53,562 --> 00:20:55,520
Wait for it to finish,
then send another query,

445
00:20:55,520 --> 00:20:58,060
then wait for it to finish,
then send another query.

446
00:20:58,060 --> 00:21:01,520
And that gives me the shortest
latency I've ever observed.

447
00:21:01,520 --> 00:21:04,080
And then with max throughput,
hit it with everything at once.

448
00:21:04,080 --> 00:21:04,920
Wait for it to finish.

449
00:21:04,920 --> 00:21:07,170
So notice how this one is
shorter, the max throughput,

450
00:21:07,170 --> 00:21:11,220
because it'll generally
finish all the requests faster

451
00:21:11,220 --> 00:21:14,260
if you give them all at once,
but any given request takes

452
00:21:14,260 --> 00:21:16,140
longer.

453
00:21:16,140 --> 00:21:22,880
So yeah, if you're
a systems of a--

454
00:21:22,880 --> 00:21:23,800
signals.

455
00:21:23,800 --> 00:21:26,960
And W type engineer think of
this as an impulse response

456
00:21:26,960 --> 00:21:30,320
that you're measuring
in the system.

457
00:21:30,320 --> 00:21:30,820
Cool.

458
00:21:30,820 --> 00:21:33,720
So to develop an
intuition for metrics,

459
00:21:33,720 --> 00:21:37,120
put out this cute little tool.

460
00:21:37,120 --> 00:21:39,720
Because you want to see
what these things look

461
00:21:39,720 --> 00:21:42,880
like to decide, oh, is
this a good experience.

462
00:21:42,880 --> 00:21:44,500
Let me zoom out a little bit.

463
00:21:44,500 --> 00:21:46,460
So this one is really fast.

464
00:21:46,460 --> 00:21:49,840
This is some optimizations
that I like measured those.

465
00:21:49,840 --> 00:21:51,620
Put them into this
little simulator.

466
00:21:51,620 --> 00:21:53,825
And this is without
those optimizations.

467
00:21:53,825 --> 00:21:56,200
And just to give you a sense
for why we care about things

468
00:21:56,200 --> 00:22:02,600
like P95 or P99
latency, like, let's

469
00:22:02,600 --> 00:22:05,920
keep the P50 latency
on the left the same

470
00:22:05,920 --> 00:22:07,620
and then boost those tails.

471
00:22:07,620 --> 00:22:10,260
So you measure something
like P95 and P99.

472
00:22:10,260 --> 00:22:12,280
You think, oh, only one
out of every 20 or one

473
00:22:12,280 --> 00:22:15,140
out of every 100 users
experiences this.

474
00:22:15,140 --> 00:22:16,500
How big of a deal could it be?

475
00:22:16,500 --> 00:22:18,360
But you're usually
measuring these things

476
00:22:18,360 --> 00:22:21,780
at the scale of a
single sequence.

477
00:22:21,780 --> 00:22:26,060
You're looking at P95 completion
of a sequence, P95 production

478
00:22:26,060 --> 00:22:27,960
of a token in this case.

479
00:22:27,960 --> 00:22:31,620
And so you can see even though
the median token comes out

480
00:22:31,620 --> 00:22:35,060
in a millisecond, in this case,
you see all this stuttering.

481
00:22:35,060 --> 00:22:37,180
And if you are probably
familiar with this

482
00:22:37,180 --> 00:22:39,280
if you've used many of
the inference providers,

483
00:22:39,280 --> 00:22:41,900
because there are frequently
these long tails that

484
00:22:41,900 --> 00:22:43,660
are hard to control.

485
00:22:43,660 --> 00:22:46,380
So I find this tool
useful for when

486
00:22:46,380 --> 00:22:50,020
I want to show people the
improvements without having

487
00:22:50,020 --> 00:22:55,480
to record traces, which
can be difficult. Cool.

488
00:22:55,480 --> 00:22:59,900
So that's on the LLM
engineer's Almanac as well.

489
00:22:59,900 --> 00:23:00,400
All right.

490
00:23:00,400 --> 00:23:02,000
So that's workloads and SLOs.

491
00:23:02,000 --> 00:23:04,680
Again, as I said earlier just
ripping through this stuff,

492
00:23:04,680 --> 00:23:07,060
so we have time for
questions about the things

493
00:23:07,060 --> 00:23:10,860
people are most
interested in at the end.

494
00:23:10,860 --> 00:23:11,400
All right.

495
00:23:11,400 --> 00:23:13,280
So models and engines.

496
00:23:13,280 --> 00:23:16,880
So in my opinion models are
also part of the workload.

497
00:23:16,880 --> 00:23:20,360
You want to-- for
inference engineering,

498
00:23:20,360 --> 00:23:22,560
you want the
application developer

499
00:23:22,560 --> 00:23:25,440
to decide running like
the transformers version

500
00:23:25,440 --> 00:23:27,280
of the model in a
Jupyter Notebook

501
00:23:27,280 --> 00:23:33,240
what model quality is necessary
to service the application.

502
00:23:33,240 --> 00:23:38,680
So it fits under the workloads,
but the model that you're using

503
00:23:38,680 --> 00:23:42,800
is one of the most important
decisions that is made.

504
00:23:42,800 --> 00:23:47,760
What I've observed is that
there are two basic families

505
00:23:47,760 --> 00:23:50,440
of constraints that lead
to very different model

506
00:23:50,440 --> 00:23:52,760
options and model choices.

507
00:23:52,760 --> 00:23:55,980
So I call them efficiency
bound and capability bound.

508
00:23:55,980 --> 00:24:00,960
Efficiency bound means that
intelligence is satisfactory.

509
00:24:00,960 --> 00:24:04,000
It's not that hard for
the trainers of models

510
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:06,960
to produce a model that
can solve this task.

511
00:24:06,960 --> 00:24:09,860
And once that happens, cost
becomes the primary driver.

512
00:24:09,860 --> 00:24:12,500
And in this domain, open models
have generally dominated.

513
00:24:12,500 --> 00:24:16,240
The proprietary providers are
not going after these workloads.

514
00:24:16,240 --> 00:24:18,940
What they're going after
instead are these capability

515
00:24:18,940 --> 00:24:21,860
bound workloads, where the
highest intelligence that

516
00:24:21,860 --> 00:24:23,940
currently exists is not enough.

517
00:24:23,940 --> 00:24:26,300
And to some extent, it
may never be enough.

518
00:24:26,300 --> 00:24:30,260
One analogy I give here is
like efficiency bound is

519
00:24:30,260 --> 00:24:32,220
like refresh rates on monitors.

520
00:24:32,220 --> 00:24:35,257
Once you hit like 60
Hertz, it's pretty good.

521
00:24:35,257 --> 00:24:36,840
You hit a hundred
Hertz, it's amazing.

522
00:24:36,840 --> 00:24:39,700
Very few people want
a thousand Hertz.

523
00:24:39,700 --> 00:24:43,660
But RAM on the other hand,
we're at 64 gigabytes

524
00:24:43,660 --> 00:24:47,040
in people's laptops, and
Cloud Code and Chrome

525
00:24:47,040 --> 00:24:48,260
keep asking for more.

526
00:24:48,260 --> 00:24:52,060
And so that's a little bit more
like this capability bound case.

527
00:24:52,060 --> 00:24:54,060
And so I bring
that up just to say

528
00:24:54,060 --> 00:24:58,780
that these are two distinct
engineering constraints that

529
00:24:58,780 --> 00:25:01,980
emerge in different parts
of computer systems.

530
00:25:01,980 --> 00:25:04,780
And so I think it's foolish
to think that one of these

531
00:25:04,780 --> 00:25:07,940
is going to be the
only one that matters,

532
00:25:07,940 --> 00:25:12,100
despite what some of the
proprietary lab people, I think,

533
00:25:12,100 --> 00:25:13,380
say.

534
00:25:13,380 --> 00:25:17,000
So the proprietary models do
dominate this capability bound

535
00:25:17,000 --> 00:25:18,480
regime.

536
00:25:18,480 --> 00:25:19,380
We'll talk about it.

537
00:25:19,380 --> 00:25:21,320
Many people want to
maybe join those labs

538
00:25:21,320 --> 00:25:22,880
and work on inference there.

539
00:25:22,880 --> 00:25:25,760
But actually, I would say fine
tunes of open source models

540
00:25:25,760 --> 00:25:29,120
are catching up here,
where maybe the frontier

541
00:25:29,120 --> 00:25:33,260
capabilities are satisfactory
but not as much as you'd like.

542
00:25:33,260 --> 00:25:35,760
And then you RL something
like DeepSeek V4,

543
00:25:35,760 --> 00:25:37,760
and all of a sudden you
have better capabilities

544
00:25:37,760 --> 00:25:40,280
and maybe you even
improve the efficiency.

545
00:25:40,280 --> 00:25:42,760
And so this only in
the last, I would say,

546
00:25:42,760 --> 00:25:45,640
three or six months has
that started to actually

547
00:25:45,640 --> 00:25:49,780
really come true as open
models have caught up

548
00:25:49,780 --> 00:25:51,520
and as more tasks have--

549
00:25:51,520 --> 00:25:54,660

550
00:25:54,660 --> 00:25:58,480
as intelligence has
increased in general.

551
00:25:58,480 --> 00:26:00,460
So when capabilities
are satisfactory,

552
00:26:00,460 --> 00:26:02,760
cost is going to be
your primary driver.

553
00:26:02,760 --> 00:26:06,262
This efficiency bound regime--
model quality is easy.

554
00:26:06,262 --> 00:26:08,720
And so the thing that you're
trying to achieve and engineer

555
00:26:08,720 --> 00:26:11,620
in the system is
cost performance,

556
00:26:11,620 --> 00:26:14,700
which is high
performance for low cost

557
00:26:14,700 --> 00:26:18,100
or adequate performance
for the lowest cost maybe.

558
00:26:18,100 --> 00:26:21,100
So many like right
now-- what this

559
00:26:21,100 --> 00:26:23,540
looks like is these are
typically single GPU per replica

560
00:26:23,540 --> 00:26:24,520
deployments.

561
00:26:24,520 --> 00:26:27,180
The models that
are pretty good are

562
00:26:27,180 --> 00:26:29,040
on the order of
billion parameters,

563
00:26:29,040 --> 00:26:31,620
10 billion parameters,
50 billion parameters

564
00:26:31,620 --> 00:26:34,980
fits inside the VRAM of
popular accelerators.

565
00:26:34,980 --> 00:26:38,100
You switch over to multiple
GPUs if you have a low latency

566
00:26:38,100 --> 00:26:39,540
budget.

567
00:26:39,540 --> 00:26:45,020
Multiple GPUs allow you to split
work across multiple memory

568
00:26:45,020 --> 00:26:47,900
pipes, and that allows you
to remove some bottlenecks

569
00:26:47,900 --> 00:26:50,220
on latency.

570
00:26:50,220 --> 00:26:52,900
But that is less
commonly the case

571
00:26:52,900 --> 00:26:54,740
because these workloads
are, at this point,

572
00:26:54,740 --> 00:26:58,080
often data processors,
subagents of background agents,

573
00:26:58,080 --> 00:27:01,060
and rarely that chatbot
plus kind of workload,

574
00:27:01,060 --> 00:27:04,980
where this human interactive
latency is really critical.

575
00:27:04,980 --> 00:27:07,340
And so it's not
as often that you

576
00:27:07,340 --> 00:27:09,200
have that super
tight latency budget.

577
00:27:09,200 --> 00:27:12,000
So maybe an exception
to this is voice agents,

578
00:27:12,000 --> 00:27:14,040
where people
yapping on the phone

579
00:27:14,040 --> 00:27:16,800
don't need to talk
to superintelligence.

580
00:27:16,800 --> 00:27:19,977
And they really want an
answer in 100 milliseconds.

581
00:27:19,977 --> 00:27:21,560
And so that's maybe
a case where we're

582
00:27:21,560 --> 00:27:24,600
starting to see some
of this multiple GPU

583
00:27:24,600 --> 00:27:28,240
for an efficiency bound case.

584
00:27:28,240 --> 00:27:32,840
The speaking of
voice models, these

585
00:27:32,840 --> 00:27:36,800
are often multimodal
input structured output.

586
00:27:36,800 --> 00:27:41,520
This is a case where the adding
additional modes gives you

587
00:27:41,520 --> 00:27:43,380
access to more
information to the model.

588
00:27:43,380 --> 00:27:45,260
But it's like a hit
on capabilities.

589
00:27:45,260 --> 00:27:47,400
If you want the absolute
highest intelligence.

590
00:27:47,400 --> 00:27:52,240
Right now, people get that
with language-only models.

591
00:27:52,240 --> 00:27:53,840
At least the open source models.

592
00:27:53,840 --> 00:27:55,800
And then the goal
is often to extract

593
00:27:55,800 --> 00:27:58,640
some kind of structured
output from this,

594
00:27:58,640 --> 00:28:00,440
so to turn unstructured
information

595
00:28:00,440 --> 00:28:02,140
into structured information.

596
00:28:02,140 --> 00:28:09,020
And this is something where you
don't need a IOI medal winning

597
00:28:09,020 --> 00:28:11,517
intelligence to do this.

598
00:28:11,517 --> 00:28:13,100
So good news about
this one is there's

599
00:28:13,100 --> 00:28:14,680
many options for
your models here--

600
00:28:14,680 --> 00:28:19,000
Quinn, Nemo Tron, GPT OSS
Gemma, Step from Stepfun,

601
00:28:19,000 --> 00:28:20,620
Mistral, all of these.

602
00:28:20,620 --> 00:28:23,680
And not only is there
this list of options here.

603
00:28:23,680 --> 00:28:28,040
They are also-- each of
these is a model family.

604
00:28:28,040 --> 00:28:29,820
So they have
different data mixes.

605
00:28:29,820 --> 00:28:31,440
So maybe they're
optimized for coding.

606
00:28:31,440 --> 00:28:33,240
Maybe they're
optimized for text.

607
00:28:33,240 --> 00:28:34,640
They have different sizes.

608
00:28:34,640 --> 00:28:36,160
They handle
different modalities.

609
00:28:36,160 --> 00:28:44,260
So this is at the point where
you're spoiled for choice,

610
00:28:44,260 --> 00:28:47,740
it feels like, in many cases.

611
00:28:47,740 --> 00:28:54,220
On the other side when
capabilities are the bottleneck,

612
00:28:54,220 --> 00:28:55,720
model quality is
really hard to hit.

613
00:28:55,720 --> 00:28:59,140
The solution to that is make
the model really, really big.

614
00:28:59,140 --> 00:29:02,900
We have only scaling as our
solutions to high intelligence

615
00:29:02,900 --> 00:29:04,300
right now.

616
00:29:04,300 --> 00:29:06,600
And so that has a
couple of consequences.

617
00:29:06,600 --> 00:29:09,960
One is these are basically
always multiple GPUs

618
00:29:09,960 --> 00:29:10,620
per replica.

619
00:29:10,620 --> 00:29:13,040
Nobody can get enough HBM
to run a trillion parameter

620
00:29:13,040 --> 00:29:17,120
MOE in a single chip.

621
00:29:17,120 --> 00:29:20,380
And maybe I shouldn't
say that so much--

622
00:29:20,380 --> 00:29:22,820
I forget how much HBM
there is in Cerebras,

623
00:29:22,820 --> 00:29:24,100
but it's a big chip.

624
00:29:24,100 --> 00:29:27,460

625
00:29:27,460 --> 00:29:29,680
And then not only multiple
GPUs per replica but also

626
00:29:29,680 --> 00:29:31,940
multiple nodes, so
multiple operating systems

627
00:29:31,940 --> 00:29:35,320
possibly on distinct machines
talking to each other.

628
00:29:35,320 --> 00:29:38,520
That comes up more often if you
have a relatively high latency

629
00:29:38,520 --> 00:29:40,120
budget.

630
00:29:40,120 --> 00:29:42,240
So like the tighter your
latency budget, the more

631
00:29:42,240 --> 00:29:45,000
you want to keep the compute
and storage all really

632
00:29:45,000 --> 00:29:47,200
nice and close to each other.

633
00:29:47,200 --> 00:29:50,760
For capability bound stuff,
it's often the chatbot plus-- so

634
00:29:50,760 --> 00:29:57,280
your ChatGPT, your Claude, or
the orchestrator of a background

635
00:29:57,280 --> 00:29:57,860
agent.

636
00:29:57,860 --> 00:30:00,240
I think I brushed over
this a little bit.

637
00:30:00,240 --> 00:30:02,480
When I say subagent
of background agent,

638
00:30:02,480 --> 00:30:06,140
a user types a prompt, and then
a whole cavalcade of things

639
00:30:06,140 --> 00:30:08,420
occurs on the back
end in something

640
00:30:08,420 --> 00:30:14,660
like a PR producing or code
review agent or whatever.

641
00:30:14,660 --> 00:30:20,100
So these are often orchestrated
as multi-agent systems, which

642
00:30:20,100 --> 00:30:22,440
have access to different
skills, different permissions,

643
00:30:22,440 --> 00:30:23,700
boundaries, whatever.

644
00:30:23,700 --> 00:30:26,220
At the top, there's often
an orchestrator agent

645
00:30:26,220 --> 00:30:28,140
that's in charge of everything.

646
00:30:28,140 --> 00:30:30,060
And that one is
where you generally

647
00:30:30,060 --> 00:30:32,820
want the highest capabilities.

648
00:30:32,820 --> 00:30:36,900
And then the things below can
often be faster, less capable

649
00:30:36,900 --> 00:30:39,500
models solving similar tasks.

650
00:30:39,500 --> 00:30:41,860
The similarity of this
to human organizations

651
00:30:41,860 --> 00:30:43,840
I will leave as an
exercise for the reader.

652
00:30:43,840 --> 00:30:46,600

653
00:30:46,600 --> 00:30:49,100
These are often very tool
call heavy because what

654
00:30:49,100 --> 00:30:50,860
uses intelligence if
it's not interacting

655
00:30:50,860 --> 00:30:54,540
with the world-- compare
Mycroft Holmes and Sherlock.

656
00:30:54,540 --> 00:30:57,360
And so they often
interact with the world.

657
00:30:57,360 --> 00:31:00,360
And not only do they-- and I
mean interact with the world.

658
00:31:00,360 --> 00:31:03,100
They don't just fire
off, do this thing.

659
00:31:03,100 --> 00:31:03,960
They do the thing.

660
00:31:03,960 --> 00:31:07,040
They observe the results
and interact with it.

661
00:31:07,040 --> 00:31:09,833
So that has
implications for prefix.

662
00:31:09,833 --> 00:31:11,000
Prefix hit rates, very high.

663
00:31:11,000 --> 00:31:13,200
Prefix hit rates, very useful.

664
00:31:13,200 --> 00:31:16,720
It also has implications
for latency.

665
00:31:16,720 --> 00:31:19,720
It means that the latency
of these tool calls

666
00:31:19,720 --> 00:31:22,080
is going to be an important
component of the operation

667
00:31:22,080 --> 00:31:22,740
of your system.

668
00:31:22,740 --> 00:31:26,320
The tool calls will range
in latency from milliseconds

669
00:31:26,320 --> 00:31:28,200
to hours.

670
00:31:28,200 --> 00:31:32,440
And so you need to
think about that.

671
00:31:32,440 --> 00:31:34,880
In my experience, people
building these applications

672
00:31:34,880 --> 00:31:37,560
often start with
a proprietary API.

673
00:31:37,560 --> 00:31:39,060
They develop the
application there,

674
00:31:39,060 --> 00:31:40,935
and then they want to
take additional control

675
00:31:40,935 --> 00:31:42,360
over the inference.

676
00:31:42,360 --> 00:31:44,760
There are fewer options
when that time comes.

677
00:31:44,760 --> 00:31:47,800
I would say DeepSeek,
Kimmy GLM minimax,

678
00:31:47,800 --> 00:31:50,440
and that's basically the
largest and most recent release

679
00:31:50,440 --> 00:31:53,680
from each of them--
or in fact, maybe,

680
00:31:53,680 --> 00:31:57,560
whichever one has most
recently released a model.

681
00:31:57,560 --> 00:31:59,620
So that's your option.

682
00:31:59,620 --> 00:32:01,598
I think once you start
doing fine tuning,

683
00:32:01,598 --> 00:32:03,140
you get a little
bit more constrained

684
00:32:03,140 --> 00:32:06,660
in being able to switch models
because the training software is

685
00:32:06,660 --> 00:32:10,720
not super portable yet,
unlike the engines,

686
00:32:10,720 --> 00:32:12,300
which we'll get to in a second.

687
00:32:12,300 --> 00:32:14,420
Useful tool for mapping
out this space that I

688
00:32:14,420 --> 00:32:16,540
like artificial analysis--

689
00:32:16,540 --> 00:32:20,420
this is like a vibe check
or keeping up with the field

690
00:32:20,420 --> 00:32:21,548
as it moves really quickly.

691
00:32:21,548 --> 00:32:23,340
They give you these
nice charts with things

692
00:32:23,340 --> 00:32:26,420
like their intelligence
index, which obviously hard

693
00:32:26,420 --> 00:32:27,640
to define intelligence.

694
00:32:27,640 --> 00:32:28,800
They give a number.

695
00:32:28,800 --> 00:32:31,040
It's correlated with
my vibe judgment,

696
00:32:31,040 --> 00:32:34,860
so I trust it as a measure
of my vibe judgment.

697
00:32:34,860 --> 00:32:37,100
And so you have that.

698
00:32:37,100 --> 00:32:39,360
This chart is one I come
back to quite a bit.

699
00:32:39,360 --> 00:32:42,100
You have that on the y-axis
and on the x-axis price

700
00:32:42,100 --> 00:32:45,300
to serve in dollars
per mega token.

701
00:32:45,300 --> 00:32:48,200
This number is based off of
proprietary inference providers.

702
00:32:48,200 --> 00:32:52,340
In my experience, beating this
is hard but not impossible.

703
00:32:52,340 --> 00:32:57,940
And yeah, matching it also
already medium hard but far

704
00:32:57,940 --> 00:33:00,760
from impossible, so long
as you have sufficient load

705
00:33:00,760 --> 00:33:04,840
to fill up your hardware.

706
00:33:04,840 --> 00:33:07,240
So we'll talk about the
performance optimization stuff

707
00:33:07,240 --> 00:33:12,440
and why that is easier now than
it was like a year or two ago.

708
00:33:12,440 --> 00:33:14,000
Actually, one of
the key components

709
00:33:14,000 --> 00:33:16,440
for why this is easier is
because of the existence

710
00:33:16,440 --> 00:33:20,720
of extremely high-quality
open source inference engines.

711
00:33:20,720 --> 00:33:23,400
So what is an inference
engine really?

712
00:33:23,400 --> 00:33:25,140
It's got a couple of components.

713
00:33:25,140 --> 00:33:28,120
There's a server
task or process that

714
00:33:28,120 --> 00:33:30,680
communicates with the
outside world that

715
00:33:30,680 --> 00:33:34,200
generally controls some
tokenization and detokenization

716
00:33:34,200 --> 00:33:34,820
work.

717
00:33:34,820 --> 00:33:39,820
Tokenization is generally
really CPU intensive.

718
00:33:39,820 --> 00:33:42,280
And so often done in
multiple processes.

719
00:33:42,280 --> 00:33:44,760
We're in Python world here.

720
00:33:44,760 --> 00:33:49,800
And detokenization,
you are mostly

721
00:33:49,800 --> 00:33:51,780
tokenizing tokens
you've generated,

722
00:33:51,780 --> 00:33:55,060
so they come a little
bit less quickly.

723
00:33:55,060 --> 00:33:59,060
And so that's why I think you
can have multiple detokenizer

724
00:33:59,060 --> 00:34:03,780
processes, but the
typical setup has one

725
00:34:03,780 --> 00:34:06,100
or far fewer than tokenizers.

726
00:34:06,100 --> 00:34:10,020
In between these is the
scheduler process on the CPU

727
00:34:10,020 --> 00:34:14,780
that orchestrates all
the work on the GPU.

728
00:34:14,780 --> 00:34:17,080
So all of the components
are really important,

729
00:34:17,080 --> 00:34:22,060
but the bottleneck and key
that you want to think about

730
00:34:22,060 --> 00:34:23,820
is the scheduler
process that defines

731
00:34:23,820 --> 00:34:25,699
and schedules work on the GPUs.

732
00:34:25,699 --> 00:34:27,540
GPU is a coprocessor.

733
00:34:27,540 --> 00:34:29,739
You are giving it work
to do, and it gives you

734
00:34:29,739 --> 00:34:31,070
the answers back later.

735
00:34:31,070 --> 00:34:32,820
You want to make sure
that there is always

736
00:34:32,820 --> 00:34:34,699
work for that thing to do.

737
00:34:34,699 --> 00:34:38,540
And that is like-- that's
the DAO of PyTorch--

738
00:34:38,540 --> 00:34:43,900
why PyTorch eager mode works
is that while the GPU is doing

739
00:34:43,900 --> 00:34:46,860
a petaflop, Python is
taking its sweet time

740
00:34:46,860 --> 00:34:52,120
of a second to decide
which kernel to run next.

741
00:34:52,120 --> 00:34:54,100
And that's fine,
as long as you know

742
00:34:54,100 --> 00:34:57,000
which kernel to run next
before the GPU finishes.

743
00:34:57,000 --> 00:35:00,040
So this is the core piece here.

744
00:35:00,040 --> 00:35:06,413
And what has emerged is the
common interchange or narrow

745
00:35:06,413 --> 00:35:08,080
waist in between what
happens on the CPU

746
00:35:08,080 --> 00:35:14,400
and what happens on the GPU
is PyTorch for the engines.

747
00:35:14,400 --> 00:35:17,660
So there are a couple
of major options--

748
00:35:17,660 --> 00:35:20,680
what people are using, like
open source options that are out

749
00:35:20,680 --> 00:35:27,240
there, TensorRT LLM from NVIDIA
built on the TensorRT Runtime.

750
00:35:27,240 --> 00:35:29,560
That's still your best
option for small models

751
00:35:29,560 --> 00:35:34,320
and small batch
sizes of small models

752
00:35:34,320 --> 00:35:37,480
but not your first choice.

753
00:35:37,480 --> 00:35:41,080
vLLM has the widest
adoption I would say.

754
00:35:41,080 --> 00:35:44,060
They were first to market
as a proper open source,

755
00:35:44,060 --> 00:35:45,580
open governance project.

756
00:35:45,580 --> 00:35:48,240
They have a bit of an
enterprise flavor at this point,

757
00:35:48,240 --> 00:35:50,600
based off of the
development and the support

758
00:35:50,600 --> 00:35:52,200
that they've received.

759
00:35:52,200 --> 00:35:56,780
And then a slightly newer
entrant but still fairly mature

760
00:35:56,780 --> 00:35:58,460
is SGLang.

761
00:35:58,460 --> 00:36:01,440
They have a strong got
to go fast feel to them,

762
00:36:01,440 --> 00:36:03,940
and they have a little bit
more of a startupy adoption

763
00:36:03,940 --> 00:36:05,740
and support set.

764
00:36:05,740 --> 00:36:09,600
So this slide just talks that
out a little bit in detail.

765
00:36:09,600 --> 00:36:13,120
With TRT-LLM, it's a
compiled C++ runtime.

766
00:36:13,120 --> 00:36:17,020
So all the stuff I was showing
you is basically in C++,

767
00:36:17,020 --> 00:36:19,140
especially once it's
out and deployed.

768
00:36:19,140 --> 00:36:22,980
So that avoids the problem
of bottlenecking on the CPU

769
00:36:22,980 --> 00:36:25,980
without having to be a smart
engineer because the code is

770
00:36:25,980 --> 00:36:29,940
just faster or being a different
kind of smart engineer.

771
00:36:29,940 --> 00:36:33,420
No hate to my C++ friends.

772
00:36:33,420 --> 00:36:37,100
NVIDIA is the sole
developer of this.

773
00:36:37,100 --> 00:36:40,060
They still release some of
their new exciting projects

774
00:36:40,060 --> 00:36:42,380
and techniques
there, but there's

775
00:36:42,380 --> 00:36:45,980
a bit-- it feels like a slightly
decreased investment in TRT-LLM

776
00:36:45,980 --> 00:36:51,200
over time in favor of them
supporting these open libraries.

777
00:36:51,200 --> 00:36:54,200
And I think that's a
general thing NVIDIA does.

778
00:36:54,200 --> 00:36:57,040
They build the software and
demonstrate to other people

779
00:36:57,040 --> 00:36:58,422
how to use their hardware.

780
00:36:58,422 --> 00:36:59,880
And then somebody
else comes along,

781
00:36:59,880 --> 00:37:02,840
and they go back to
making their money off

782
00:37:02,840 --> 00:37:05,280
of selling those chips.

783
00:37:05,280 --> 00:37:07,140
But again, still some benefits.

784
00:37:07,140 --> 00:37:12,000
VLLM, the big
thing here, this is

785
00:37:12,000 --> 00:37:16,800
like a proper open governance,
like Linux Foundation umbrella

786
00:37:16,800 --> 00:37:20,640
project, like
Postgres or PyTorch.

787
00:37:20,640 --> 00:37:24,440
And that has implications for
the manner in which you join it

788
00:37:24,440 --> 00:37:28,280
and what you might
expect the future of it,

789
00:37:28,280 --> 00:37:29,800
how it might develop.

790
00:37:29,800 --> 00:37:32,040
SGLang on the other hand,
right now, it's open source

791
00:37:32,040 --> 00:37:33,100
but closed governance.

792
00:37:33,100 --> 00:37:35,000
There's a specific
nonprofit that

793
00:37:35,000 --> 00:37:39,000
owns this on behalf
of the maintainers

794
00:37:39,000 --> 00:37:41,240
or composed of the maintainers.

795
00:37:41,240 --> 00:37:43,300
So that's like maybe--

796
00:37:43,300 --> 00:37:44,800
struggling to think
of a good closed

797
00:37:44,800 --> 00:37:46,840
governance, open source
project that doesn't

798
00:37:46,840 --> 00:37:51,420
have a big company behind it.

799
00:37:51,420 --> 00:37:57,100
And they have spun out this
startup to develop many--

800
00:37:57,100 --> 00:37:58,480
the founding team.

801
00:37:58,480 --> 00:38:00,640
Anyway, so we'll see how
these two things develop.

802
00:38:00,640 --> 00:38:01,840
I'll say vLLM and SGLang--

803
00:38:01,840 --> 00:38:06,460
I'd say SGLang has this very
more extremely performance

804
00:38:06,460 --> 00:38:08,160
focused engineering culture.

805
00:38:08,160 --> 00:38:11,560
There's 50 bajillion
different versions of SGLang,

806
00:38:11,560 --> 00:38:14,360
as Docker images, as
branches or whatever,

807
00:38:14,360 --> 00:38:17,140
and they each have tiny
little optimizations in them.

808
00:38:17,140 --> 00:38:21,500
Whereas the LLM gets things
into main a little bit more.

809
00:38:21,500 --> 00:38:25,620
And so there's less of
that absurd hackery.

810
00:38:25,620 --> 00:38:28,540
This is my experience
working with them.

811
00:38:28,540 --> 00:38:31,900
So yeah, I haven't
found a strong reason

812
00:38:31,900 --> 00:38:34,340
to prefer one or the other yet.

813
00:38:34,340 --> 00:38:35,720
They both do great work.

814
00:38:35,720 --> 00:38:38,760
And as people who mostly
consume these kinds of engines,

815
00:38:38,760 --> 00:38:41,120
it's nice to have more suitors.

816
00:38:41,120 --> 00:38:44,217

817
00:38:44,217 --> 00:38:46,300
You're going to be serving--
you don't necessarily

818
00:38:46,300 --> 00:38:48,433
want to write your
own engine, but you

819
00:38:48,433 --> 00:38:49,600
do want to get to know them.

820
00:38:49,600 --> 00:38:51,480
There's a nice
project mini and nano

821
00:38:51,480 --> 00:38:53,887
vLLM that have simple
implementations of engines.

822
00:38:53,887 --> 00:38:55,220
Help you learn the architecture.

823
00:38:55,220 --> 00:38:57,147
Alexa Gaudich did a great
walkthrough of vLLM.

824
00:38:57,147 --> 00:38:58,480
And if you check out the slides.

825
00:38:58,480 --> 00:39:00,600
I put them on a
notebook on Modal

826
00:39:00,600 --> 00:39:02,840
so you can run them
on multiple GPUs

827
00:39:02,840 --> 00:39:05,400
and see how disaggregated
prefilled decode works

828
00:39:05,400 --> 00:39:07,120
in a notebook.

829
00:39:07,120 --> 00:39:09,980
Another useful resource, I
really Deep Wiki from Cognition.

830
00:39:09,980 --> 00:39:12,200
It's a bunch of indexed
software repositories

831
00:39:12,200 --> 00:39:14,120
that you can ask questions.

832
00:39:14,120 --> 00:39:17,400
It's nice because you don't
have to have the code downloaded

833
00:39:17,400 --> 00:39:19,900
in the place where an agent is
running where you're typing.

834
00:39:19,900 --> 00:39:21,600
You can just look
it up on your phone

835
00:39:21,600 --> 00:39:23,660
and just be like,
hey, wait a second.

836
00:39:23,660 --> 00:39:28,840
How does the radix cache in
SGLang support hybrid models?

837
00:39:28,840 --> 00:39:32,600
I think it doesn't, but
Deep Wiki would tell you.

838
00:39:32,600 --> 00:39:33,700
And it's got diagrams.

839
00:39:33,700 --> 00:39:34,340
It's organized.

840
00:39:34,340 --> 00:39:35,140
It's queryable.

841
00:39:35,140 --> 00:39:37,190
It's more than just
having the code,

842
00:39:37,190 --> 00:39:39,440
but then it's always really
nice to also have the code

843
00:39:39,440 --> 00:39:43,220
and explore it with an agent.

844
00:39:43,220 --> 00:39:45,320
Use your own thinking
tokens, not just

845
00:39:45,320 --> 00:39:48,900
the ones you're paying for.

846
00:39:48,900 --> 00:39:49,440
OK.

847
00:39:49,440 --> 00:39:51,238
So models and engines--

848
00:39:51,238 --> 00:39:52,280
so you've got your model.

849
00:39:52,280 --> 00:39:53,400
You've got your engine.

850
00:39:53,400 --> 00:39:54,733
Where are you going to run this?

851
00:39:54,733 --> 00:39:56,880
This has been a specter
that has been haunting us,

852
00:39:56,880 --> 00:39:59,620
the Specter of what does
this hardware look like.

853
00:39:59,620 --> 00:40:01,580
One thing I will say
is it feels like there

854
00:40:01,580 --> 00:40:04,720
are two workloads here from
the hardware perspective.

855
00:40:04,720 --> 00:40:07,200
You have the prefill
part and the decode part.

856
00:40:07,200 --> 00:40:10,762
So in prefill, you have some
large number of input tokens.

857
00:40:10,762 --> 00:40:11,720
Here there's only four.

858
00:40:11,720 --> 00:40:15,140
But usually, these days in
almost all the workloads

859
00:40:15,140 --> 00:40:17,180
we're talking about
here, it's hundreds,

860
00:40:17,180 --> 00:40:20,140
thousands, tens of thousands,
hundreds of thousands.

861
00:40:20,140 --> 00:40:21,420
And so that comes in.

862
00:40:21,420 --> 00:40:23,420
And in order to
process them, you're

863
00:40:23,420 --> 00:40:25,460
going to move many
gigabytes of weights

864
00:40:25,460 --> 00:40:29,260
out of the memory
of your accelerator

865
00:40:29,260 --> 00:40:32,100
into registers to
calculate something.

866
00:40:32,100 --> 00:40:36,980
And then you have to move
all the weights in order

867
00:40:36,980 --> 00:40:41,100
to calculate that or all
the active parameters.

868
00:40:41,100 --> 00:40:44,140
And then once you then
move over to-- that's

869
00:40:44,140 --> 00:40:47,500
the prefill or the prompt
processing or the input phase.

870
00:40:47,500 --> 00:40:50,940
During output phase, every
time you create a token,

871
00:40:50,940 --> 00:40:53,660
you also need to take all
the weights out of memory,

872
00:40:53,660 --> 00:40:57,720
put them into registers, and
do a calculation with them.

873
00:40:57,720 --> 00:41:04,080
And per rough rule of thumb, per
batch element-- so per token,

874
00:41:04,080 --> 00:41:08,550
per request, you're probably
going to do on the order of two

875
00:41:08,550 --> 00:41:10,120
or three floating
point operations.

876
00:41:10,120 --> 00:41:12,440
Multiply with something
and accumulate it

877
00:41:12,440 --> 00:41:14,360
into something else.

878
00:41:14,360 --> 00:41:15,820
So why does this matter?

879
00:41:15,820 --> 00:41:18,120
So that suggests that
in the decode phase

880
00:41:18,120 --> 00:41:22,440
we're doing a few
operations per byte loaded.

881
00:41:22,440 --> 00:41:26,640
And the bad news is that the
trend of hardware, like in GPUs

882
00:41:26,640 --> 00:41:32,400
and elsewhere, is for increasing
ridge point arithmetic

883
00:41:32,400 --> 00:41:33,600
intensity.

884
00:41:33,600 --> 00:41:37,902
The point at which you are
using all of the arithmetic

885
00:41:37,902 --> 00:41:39,360
bandwidth that you
have, and that's

886
00:41:39,360 --> 00:41:42,240
the limit on your performance.

887
00:41:42,240 --> 00:41:46,700
And because the memory bandwidth
is generally so much lower,

888
00:41:46,700 --> 00:41:49,860
you end up slowed.

889
00:41:49,860 --> 00:41:53,580
You aren't able to achieve
that actual full arithmetic

890
00:41:53,580 --> 00:41:54,620
bandwidth.

891
00:41:54,620 --> 00:41:56,820
So some representative
numbers down here

892
00:41:56,820 --> 00:41:59,660
for the tensor
cores of a variety

893
00:41:59,660 --> 00:42:02,240
of different recent NVIDIA GPUs.

894
00:42:02,240 --> 00:42:03,420
They're in the hundreds--

895
00:42:03,420 --> 00:42:05,520
maybe a thousand
operations per byte.

896
00:42:05,520 --> 00:42:07,820
And during decode, you're
maybe doing a couple

897
00:42:07,820 --> 00:42:10,180
of operations per byte.

898
00:42:10,180 --> 00:42:15,340
And so you are going
to struggle to make

899
00:42:15,340 --> 00:42:18,420
full use of the accelerator.

900
00:42:18,420 --> 00:42:22,320
It's costing you the same
amount during that time.

901
00:42:22,320 --> 00:42:26,220

902
00:42:26,220 --> 00:42:31,620
So you're going to end up
thinking about optimizing those

903
00:42:31,620 --> 00:42:32,805
separately from one another.

904
00:42:32,805 --> 00:42:34,180
That's why they
already showed up

905
00:42:34,180 --> 00:42:38,300
in the definition of our
applications and our workloads.

906
00:42:38,300 --> 00:42:42,800
In order to do this well,
I think the only option

907
00:42:42,800 --> 00:42:45,680
these days, in my
experience, is recent data

908
00:42:45,680 --> 00:42:50,040
center and Tensor
Core SXM NVIDIA GPUs.

909
00:42:50,040 --> 00:42:53,180
So these are marketing terms, so
let me break that down for you.

910
00:42:53,180 --> 00:42:55,680
Data centers-- what
NVIDIA uses to talk

911
00:42:55,680 --> 00:43:00,553
about the interconnect
housing, et cetera of the GPUs.

912
00:43:00,553 --> 00:43:01,720
So we see in the top right--

913
00:43:01,720 --> 00:43:06,160
I believe that's a
H100 SXM machine.

914
00:43:06,160 --> 00:43:09,100
Yeah, but it might be a B20.

915
00:43:09,100 --> 00:43:11,198
But the point is
a couple of things

916
00:43:11,198 --> 00:43:13,740
you can notice here-- it's a
little hard to see on the slide,

917
00:43:13,740 --> 00:43:15,560
so let me go back here.

918
00:43:15,560 --> 00:43:17,200
The little blue
blocks there, that's

919
00:43:17,200 --> 00:43:18,540
the high bandwidth memory.

920
00:43:18,540 --> 00:43:24,120
This is on substrate
but off chip memory.

921
00:43:24,120 --> 00:43:25,340
It's like soldered in place.

922
00:43:25,340 --> 00:43:27,798
It's not like the RAM you have
at home that you can plug-in

923
00:43:27,798 --> 00:43:28,560
and out.

924
00:43:28,560 --> 00:43:31,440
And that ultra low latency
and ultra high bandwidth

925
00:43:31,440 --> 00:43:37,320
are really critical for the
decode phase, especially.

926
00:43:37,320 --> 00:43:39,840
So you really want that.

927
00:43:39,840 --> 00:43:42,100
And that's one reason why--

928
00:43:42,100 --> 00:43:46,980
and not like GDDR
memory-based NVIDIA GPUs.

929
00:43:46,980 --> 00:43:49,940
This also is going to
give you higher capacity

930
00:43:49,940 --> 00:43:51,120
in memory in general.

931
00:43:51,120 --> 00:43:53,777
I think that's just an artifact
of how these things have

932
00:43:53,777 --> 00:43:54,360
been deployed.

933
00:43:54,360 --> 00:43:57,380
I don't think it's impossible
to build a chip with a giant GER

934
00:43:57,380 --> 00:43:57,960
RAM stack.

935
00:43:57,960 --> 00:44:00,980
I just don't think that
NVIDIA offers that.

936
00:44:00,980 --> 00:44:03,380
But let me know if I'm wrong.

937
00:44:03,380 --> 00:44:05,600
SXM is the mounting form factor.

938
00:44:05,600 --> 00:44:09,420
This is replacing PCIe, the
little finger like toothbrush

939
00:44:09,420 --> 00:44:13,380
thing at the bottom of a GPU,
the GPUs you've probably seen.

940
00:44:13,380 --> 00:44:16,580
You can see the connector
there at the arrow.

941
00:44:16,580 --> 00:44:20,600
So this allows for
better power delivery.

942
00:44:20,600 --> 00:44:25,500
And so this is where you'll
get the full wattage of GPUs.

943
00:44:25,500 --> 00:44:28,340
Where do these limitations on
memory bandwidth and arithmetic

944
00:44:28,340 --> 00:44:29,355
bandwidth come from?

945
00:44:29,355 --> 00:44:30,980
Fundamentally, they
come from the power

946
00:44:30,980 --> 00:44:32,813
that's delivered to the
chip and the ability

947
00:44:32,813 --> 00:44:35,700
to egress the heat
that it produces.

948
00:44:35,700 --> 00:44:39,640
And SXM, this mounting
factor, gives better power

949
00:44:39,640 --> 00:44:43,200
delivery and better
installation in data centers,

950
00:44:43,200 --> 00:44:48,560
and so the fastest chips will
have this somewhere in their SKU

951
00:44:48,560 --> 00:44:49,320
name.

952
00:44:49,320 --> 00:44:52,000
And then finally, yeah,
NVLink and InfiniBand

953
00:44:52,000 --> 00:44:55,600
for connecting GPUs and
connecting nodes to each other.

954
00:44:55,600 --> 00:44:59,880
I'm not going to talk too
much about that today.

955
00:44:59,880 --> 00:45:02,560
Then Tensor Core-- this will
show up in the names of GPUs

956
00:45:02,560 --> 00:45:03,220
as well.

957
00:45:03,220 --> 00:45:06,880
Tensor Cores are essentially a
matmul ASIC inside of the GPU

958
00:45:06,880 --> 00:45:11,600
that NVIDIA stapled on
after Google made the TPU.

959
00:45:11,600 --> 00:45:13,700
That's going to start
a bunch of fights.

960
00:45:13,700 --> 00:45:18,920
But let's say this is
essentially a distinct hardware

961
00:45:18,920 --> 00:45:22,320
unit programmed distinctly
from the rest of the GPU.

962
00:45:22,320 --> 00:45:24,640
And it is where 99%
of the FLOPs are

963
00:45:24,640 --> 00:45:27,600
in your contemporary hardware.

964
00:45:27,600 --> 00:45:34,520
And it is only able to do
matrix-matrix multiplication.

965
00:45:34,520 --> 00:45:39,120
So hot tip-- if you're thinking
about weird model architectures,

966
00:45:39,120 --> 00:45:42,620
make sure that they can take
advantage of matrix matrix

967
00:45:42,620 --> 00:45:43,560
multiplication.

968
00:45:43,560 --> 00:45:45,283
Don't make the
transformers mistake

969
00:45:45,283 --> 00:45:46,700
of having your
inference look like

970
00:45:46,700 --> 00:45:50,740
matrix-vector multiplication.

971
00:45:50,740 --> 00:45:53,380
Quick, shameless
plug here for if you

972
00:45:53,380 --> 00:45:57,100
want an intro to a lot of
the terms that I just used.

973
00:45:57,100 --> 00:45:59,860
Wrote this GPU glossary.

974
00:45:59,860 --> 00:46:01,980
Collects everything
from warp schedulers up

975
00:46:01,980 --> 00:46:04,177
to compiler flags in one place.

976
00:46:04,177 --> 00:46:06,260
Also a bunch of stuff on
performance optimization,

977
00:46:06,260 --> 00:46:09,240
like that ridge point diagram.

978
00:46:09,240 --> 00:46:11,160
So that's book knowledge.

979
00:46:11,160 --> 00:46:13,340
If you want a little bit
more street knowledge,

980
00:46:13,340 --> 00:46:16,680
tensara.org that a
friend of mine made,

981
00:46:16,680 --> 00:46:21,100
it's a leak cheat code
for GPU programming.

982
00:46:21,100 --> 00:46:23,540
And yeah, I guess
it's a shameless plug

983
00:46:23,540 --> 00:46:26,780
because it runs on Moodle.

984
00:46:26,780 --> 00:46:27,280
OK.

985
00:46:27,280 --> 00:46:28,940
So I jumped right into
talking about GPUs

986
00:46:28,940 --> 00:46:31,023
without talking about what
your other options are.

987
00:46:31,023 --> 00:46:34,300
I think right now CPUs generally
can't achieve acceptable latency

988
00:46:34,300 --> 00:46:37,240
for the models that
people want to run.

989
00:46:37,240 --> 00:46:40,440
You really need
HBM, so that makes

990
00:46:40,440 --> 00:46:44,560
it hard to use most CPUs because
they're deployed with RAM

991
00:46:44,560 --> 00:46:46,160
and processor distinct.

992
00:46:46,160 --> 00:46:48,200
These things do exist.

993
00:46:48,200 --> 00:46:52,680
There were some troubles with
Intel's HBM being able to feed--

994
00:46:52,680 --> 00:46:56,200
the HBM and the CPU being able
to keep pace with each other.

995
00:46:56,200 --> 00:46:59,480
One big problem here is that
GPUs give the programmer control

996
00:46:59,480 --> 00:47:02,680
of the L1 cache
SM shared memory,

997
00:47:02,680 --> 00:47:04,560
and that's really
important for being

998
00:47:04,560 --> 00:47:09,200
able to make use of very
high arithmetic bandwidth.

999
00:47:09,200 --> 00:47:12,685
And yeah, so people
are doing a lot

1000
00:47:12,685 --> 00:47:14,060
of really excellent
work on this,

1001
00:47:14,060 --> 00:47:15,685
so I'd expect this
to change over time.

1002
00:47:15,685 --> 00:47:17,200
And models will get smaller.

1003
00:47:17,200 --> 00:47:18,260
Models get smaller.

1004
00:47:18,260 --> 00:47:19,020
CPUs get bigger.

1005
00:47:19,020 --> 00:47:20,520
This will cross
over, and there will

1006
00:47:20,520 --> 00:47:22,360
be a lot of really
awesome CPU inference

1007
00:47:22,360 --> 00:47:24,120
happening but not today.

1008
00:47:24,120 --> 00:47:25,450
AMD GPUs-- I don't use--

1009
00:47:25,450 --> 00:47:26,700
I haven't used them very much.

1010
00:47:26,700 --> 00:47:30,420
I've heard the programming on
them is somewhat challenging,

1011
00:47:30,420 --> 00:47:32,900
and achieving the full-rated
performance is challenging,

1012
00:47:32,900 --> 00:47:35,240
but they've also been doing
a lot of excellent work,

1013
00:47:35,240 --> 00:47:37,180
so watch that space.

1014
00:47:37,180 --> 00:47:39,380
TPUs-- I personally
am a little spooked

1015
00:47:39,380 --> 00:47:42,160
by the idea of having only
one supplier end to end.

1016
00:47:42,160 --> 00:47:47,060
You can buy corals--
little tiny like TPUs.

1017
00:47:47,060 --> 00:47:50,980
But I have a 2070 that I put
under my pillow at night.

1018
00:47:50,980 --> 00:47:53,940
And I feel like I
know that machine.

1019
00:47:53,940 --> 00:47:55,520
And like a TPU, I don't.

1020
00:47:55,520 --> 00:47:58,420
And then there's the more
seriously economic concerns

1021
00:47:58,420 --> 00:48:02,060
of purchasing from such a
tightly integrated monopoly as

1022
00:48:02,060 --> 00:48:06,180
opposed to a loosely integrated
monopoly with NVIDIA.

1023
00:48:06,180 --> 00:48:09,238
There are other inference
chips on the horizon.

1024
00:48:09,238 --> 00:48:11,780
They're generally harder-- they
have the same single supplier

1025
00:48:11,780 --> 00:48:13,940
program in general.

1026
00:48:13,940 --> 00:48:15,240
Many of them have walked back.

1027
00:48:15,240 --> 00:48:17,032
They're like, oh, buy
a chip and install it

1028
00:48:17,032 --> 00:48:19,060
in your own racks thing.

1029
00:48:19,060 --> 00:48:23,260
And they generally don't
deploy via the hyperscalers.

1030
00:48:23,260 --> 00:48:27,020
And the cloud experience
of programming

1031
00:48:27,020 --> 00:48:28,860
them is like with TPUs.

1032
00:48:28,860 --> 00:48:29,840
They built it.

1033
00:48:29,840 --> 00:48:31,920
Then you use it via their cloud.

1034
00:48:31,920 --> 00:48:35,780
That's allowing them to move
faster, so no shade on that.

1035
00:48:35,780 --> 00:48:40,940
But this also makes them harder
to program lower installed base.

1036
00:48:40,940 --> 00:48:46,480
There are not like 13-year-old
basement dweller hackers reverse

1037
00:48:46,480 --> 00:48:50,860
engineering the instruction set,
like there is with NVIDIA GPUs.

1038
00:48:50,860 --> 00:48:52,460
So you don't have that breadth.

1039
00:48:52,460 --> 00:48:54,585
So that makes me a little
bit concerned about them,

1040
00:48:54,585 --> 00:48:58,480
but it's also very clear that
there's a lot going on there.

1041
00:48:58,480 --> 00:48:59,060
All right.

1042
00:48:59,060 --> 00:49:00,060
So that's hardware.

1043
00:49:00,060 --> 00:49:02,740
So we don't just need hardware.

1044
00:49:02,740 --> 00:49:06,440
We need to plug it in to things
like power and networking

1045
00:49:06,440 --> 00:49:08,978
and get things running on it.

1046
00:49:08,978 --> 00:49:10,520
Your deployment
options are basically

1047
00:49:10,520 --> 00:49:11,820
constrained by the hardware.

1048
00:49:11,820 --> 00:49:13,500
So GPUs and HBM are scarce.

1049
00:49:13,500 --> 00:49:15,625
They're likely to remain scarce.

1050
00:49:15,625 --> 00:49:17,000
And so because of
that, inference

1051
00:49:17,000 --> 00:49:18,782
is mostly served
right now, not local.

1052
00:49:18,782 --> 00:49:21,240
Most inference applications
you're going to build right now

1053
00:49:21,240 --> 00:49:24,760
are going to run in a data
center on behalf of clients.

1054
00:49:24,760 --> 00:49:27,000
I do expect that to change--

1055
00:49:27,000 --> 00:49:30,640
another watch this space
compared to databases.

1056
00:49:30,640 --> 00:49:32,980
How many SQLite databases
do you think there

1057
00:49:32,980 --> 00:49:36,660
are versus Postgres databases?

1058
00:49:36,660 --> 00:49:41,180
There's trillions
of SQLite databases.

1059
00:49:41,180 --> 00:49:42,640
Somebody calculated it.

1060
00:49:42,640 --> 00:49:47,700
So eventually, things will go
out to the edge substantially.

1061
00:49:47,700 --> 00:49:51,100
But for now, cloud deployment--

1062
00:49:51,100 --> 00:49:54,620
ultra low latency budgets are
generally eaten into by network.

1063
00:49:54,620 --> 00:49:57,300
So you're losing on the
order of tens of milliseconds

1064
00:49:57,300 --> 00:50:01,260
if you have regional deployments
or hundreds of milliseconds

1065
00:50:01,260 --> 00:50:03,220
if you have global deployments.

1066
00:50:03,220 --> 00:50:06,260
So that increases the
pressure on latency

1067
00:50:06,260 --> 00:50:08,700
and pushes you to these
more complex regional

1068
00:50:08,700 --> 00:50:12,460
or edge in the data
center sense deployments.

1069
00:50:12,460 --> 00:50:15,640
And then because these things
are scarce and in demand,

1070
00:50:15,640 --> 00:50:17,283
they're expensive.

1071
00:50:17,283 --> 00:50:18,700
And so you're going
to be focusing

1072
00:50:18,700 --> 00:50:22,600
a lot on maximizing what you're
getting out of the hardware.

1073
00:50:22,600 --> 00:50:26,060
This has maybe been-- this is
like the implicit driver behind

1074
00:50:26,060 --> 00:50:28,680
how much we've been concerned
about performance in this entire

1075
00:50:28,680 --> 00:50:29,540
discussion--

1076
00:50:29,540 --> 00:50:32,640

1077
00:50:32,640 --> 00:50:37,120
the cost of an employee to have
an 8x B200 machine running.

1078
00:50:37,120 --> 00:50:41,960
So people care
about performance.

1079
00:50:41,960 --> 00:50:44,720
Another problem--
GPUs fail a lot.

1080
00:50:44,720 --> 00:50:48,560
So mean time to failure,
NVIDIA H100s measured

1081
00:50:48,560 --> 00:50:52,480
in weeks or days, not years.

1082
00:50:52,480 --> 00:50:56,560
So if you read war stories
from older engineers

1083
00:50:56,560 --> 00:50:58,880
about working with spinning
disks, it's more like that.

1084
00:50:58,880 --> 00:51:01,520
You have to build in an
assumption of failure

1085
00:51:01,520 --> 00:51:06,200
and therefore like redundancy,
so that faults in hardware

1086
00:51:06,200 --> 00:51:07,822
don't become failures
in your system.

1087
00:51:07,822 --> 00:51:09,280
The good news is
if you're building

1088
00:51:09,280 --> 00:51:11,860
an inference system your
life is a little bit easier.

1089
00:51:11,860 --> 00:51:15,660
Training requires basically
all GPUs to be up at all times,

1090
00:51:15,660 --> 00:51:20,480
or else the whole world stops
because all the replicas

1091
00:51:20,480 --> 00:51:21,380
are independent.

1092
00:51:21,380 --> 00:51:24,860
You just route to a different
replica and keep going.

1093
00:51:24,860 --> 00:51:27,800
But it is a concern about
robustness, reliability,

1094
00:51:27,800 --> 00:51:30,155
and cost performance.

1095
00:51:30,155 --> 00:51:32,280
So we wrote a little bit
about what we've observed.

1096
00:51:32,280 --> 00:51:34,300
I'm not going to go in great
detail, but check out this blog

1097
00:51:34,300 --> 00:51:36,592
if you want to learn a little
bit about what this looks

1098
00:51:36,592 --> 00:51:38,180
like when you're
looking at thousands

1099
00:51:38,180 --> 00:51:40,480
or low tens of
thousands of GPUs.

1100
00:51:40,480 --> 00:51:42,380
You could see different
cloud providers have

1101
00:51:42,380 --> 00:51:44,640
very different failure
rates-- for instance,

1102
00:51:44,640 --> 00:51:46,880
different deployments.

1103
00:51:46,880 --> 00:51:48,820
Some are better than others.

1104
00:51:48,820 --> 00:51:52,820
And there are things you
can do to work on this.

1105
00:51:52,820 --> 00:51:54,900
But the bigger challenge
with inference serving

1106
00:51:54,900 --> 00:51:56,960
is variability in traffic.

1107
00:51:56,960 --> 00:51:58,300
So this is simulated.

1108
00:51:58,300 --> 00:52:00,900
But what we typically see
with inference applications

1109
00:52:00,900 --> 00:52:03,500
on our platform is that
over a period of time

1110
00:52:03,500 --> 00:52:05,120
the request per
minute is variable.

1111
00:52:05,120 --> 00:52:07,260
There's seasonal
variability, but there's also

1112
00:52:07,260 --> 00:52:10,760
very big swings in demand.

1113
00:52:10,760 --> 00:52:14,140
I think this is partly
due the fastest growing

1114
00:52:14,140 --> 00:52:16,440
companies in history are
being created right now.

1115
00:52:16,440 --> 00:52:19,140
We have social media to
distribute products really

1116
00:52:19,140 --> 00:52:20,500
quickly.

1117
00:52:20,500 --> 00:52:23,760
And so there's a number of
dynamics that lead to this.

1118
00:52:23,760 --> 00:52:25,740
And with inference as
opposed to training,

1119
00:52:25,740 --> 00:52:27,948
you don't like engineer and
plan around these things.

1120
00:52:27,948 --> 00:52:29,880
They just happen to you.

1121
00:52:29,880 --> 00:52:34,760
So key thing that
we work on at Modal

1122
00:52:34,760 --> 00:52:38,840
is trying to allow you to
provision only the GPUs

1123
00:52:38,840 --> 00:52:41,980
that you want or that you
need at any given time.

1124
00:52:41,980 --> 00:52:44,060
The default thing would
be to just buy them.

1125
00:52:44,060 --> 00:52:45,312
Maybe rack them ahead of time.

1126
00:52:45,312 --> 00:52:47,520
And then you have to guess
how big your peak is going

1127
00:52:47,520 --> 00:52:49,660
to be, and then
anytime off peak,

1128
00:52:49,660 --> 00:52:51,820
you're still paying
for the hardware,

1129
00:52:51,820 --> 00:52:54,280
whether that's directly in
the form of cloud rental

1130
00:52:54,280 --> 00:52:57,560
or indirectly as
amortized costs.

1131
00:52:57,560 --> 00:53:00,460
So this is madly inefficient.

1132
00:53:00,460 --> 00:53:03,520
If you look online
or in this blog post,

1133
00:53:03,520 --> 00:53:06,820
people report like 30%,
40% utilization here.

1134
00:53:06,820 --> 00:53:10,640
So these are made
up numbers, but they

1135
00:53:10,640 --> 00:53:14,120
are tended to be true to life.

1136
00:53:14,120 --> 00:53:15,500
So solution-- OK.

1137
00:53:15,500 --> 00:53:17,340
Well, let me just get
GPUs as I need them.

1138
00:53:17,340 --> 00:53:18,840
The problem is that
it's really hard

1139
00:53:18,840 --> 00:53:22,200
to make the startup of new
replicas of inference servers

1140
00:53:22,200 --> 00:53:23,620
really fast.

1141
00:53:23,620 --> 00:53:26,660
And so if you don't engineer
this part of your system

1142
00:53:26,660 --> 00:53:30,260
carefully, you can have poor
utilization and poor quality

1143
00:53:30,260 --> 00:53:31,040
of service.

1144
00:53:31,040 --> 00:53:33,260
You both have GPUs
you're not using

1145
00:53:33,260 --> 00:53:35,220
that have stuck around
after you spin them up,

1146
00:53:35,220 --> 00:53:39,020
and you will miss peaks.

1147
00:53:39,020 --> 00:53:41,660
So the goal is to achieve
fast and automatic allocation

1148
00:53:41,660 --> 00:53:43,420
so that you can have
maximal utilization

1149
00:53:43,420 --> 00:53:45,140
and good quality of service.

1150
00:53:45,140 --> 00:53:49,000
So for interest of time, I
won't go through this in detail.

1151
00:53:49,000 --> 00:53:53,260
There's an extremely detailed
blog post about this, about some

1152
00:53:53,260 --> 00:53:55,260
of the approaches
that we took-- talking

1153
00:53:55,260 --> 00:53:59,060
about the actual internals and
hopes that other folks can learn

1154
00:53:59,060 --> 00:54:00,260
from them.

1155
00:54:00,260 --> 00:54:03,160
So the basic idea
is, number one,

1156
00:54:03,160 --> 00:54:04,820
don't spin up a
machine from scratch

1157
00:54:04,820 --> 00:54:06,540
just because a user
request came in.

1158
00:54:06,540 --> 00:54:08,940
Operate a buffer
of idle machines

1159
00:54:08,940 --> 00:54:12,900
ready to go to handle new
traffic as things change.

1160
00:54:12,900 --> 00:54:15,918
So have a couple spare.

1161
00:54:15,918 --> 00:54:17,460
This is particularly
important if you

1162
00:54:17,460 --> 00:54:20,840
have multiple applications
scaling up on the same pool.

1163
00:54:20,840 --> 00:54:24,080
So if you're running multiple
different deployments,

1164
00:54:24,080 --> 00:54:28,160
you might not know which one is
going to need to scale up next.

1165
00:54:28,160 --> 00:54:31,220
So this one-- we're massively
a multi-tenant system,

1166
00:54:31,220 --> 00:54:33,680
so this is one we
think about a lot.

1167
00:54:33,680 --> 00:54:35,800
Check out the blog
post for details

1168
00:54:35,800 --> 00:54:40,040
on the way we solve this with
classic operations, research,

1169
00:54:40,040 --> 00:54:44,280
linear solver approach,
managing the size of that buffer

1170
00:54:44,280 --> 00:54:48,240
and taking into account price.

1171
00:54:48,240 --> 00:54:50,840
So that one is really important
for multi-tenant systems,

1172
00:54:50,840 --> 00:54:52,560
especially--

1173
00:54:52,560 --> 00:54:55,200
But for all systems, if
you're creating and destroying

1174
00:54:55,200 --> 00:54:58,840
replicas, you're probably using
something like a container, VM.

1175
00:54:58,840 --> 00:55:02,640
You need to get the files that
are necessary for that thing

1176
00:55:02,640 --> 00:55:05,320
to run onto the system
from somewhere--

1177
00:55:05,320 --> 00:55:07,200
somewhere in cloud storage.

1178
00:55:07,200 --> 00:55:11,232
Our solution is, one, get
the loading of the whole file

1179
00:55:11,232 --> 00:55:12,940
system out of the way
of container start.

1180
00:55:12,940 --> 00:55:16,040
So lazily load the
file system, but then

1181
00:55:16,040 --> 00:55:18,792
also eagerly load
the first things

1182
00:55:18,792 --> 00:55:20,000
that are going to be needed--

1183
00:55:20,000 --> 00:55:23,940
PyTorch, Python Core
operating system libraries.

1184
00:55:23,940 --> 00:55:27,380
Fetch those things concurrently
with the container replica

1185
00:55:27,380 --> 00:55:32,600
starting, and then store those
in a multi-tier cloud cache.

1186
00:55:32,600 --> 00:55:35,380
Everything from the Linux
page cache on a replica,

1187
00:55:35,380 --> 00:55:38,940
all the way up to blob storage
with all kinds of things

1188
00:55:38,940 --> 00:55:42,560
in between that can
deliver that faster.

1189
00:55:42,560 --> 00:55:48,180
Again, the full version here
is important for a wide variety

1190
00:55:48,180 --> 00:55:51,500
of inference workloads or
a multi-tenant environment,

1191
00:55:51,500 --> 00:55:53,140
but these same
basic technologies

1192
00:55:53,140 --> 00:55:54,940
are what you're
likely to use even

1193
00:55:54,940 --> 00:55:59,500
in a single tenant deployment
or even a single workload.

1194
00:55:59,500 --> 00:56:03,340
Then you want to
start the application.

1195
00:56:03,340 --> 00:56:06,620
And this takes minutes for
things like SGLang and VLM.

1196
00:56:06,620 --> 00:56:08,280
They do just in
time compilation.

1197
00:56:08,280 --> 00:56:09,520
They do CUDA graph capture.

1198
00:56:09,520 --> 00:56:11,160
They do Torch compilation.

1199
00:56:11,160 --> 00:56:14,220
All of these things
take time, and it

1200
00:56:14,220 --> 00:56:17,600
seems like this is something
that you just have to wait for.

1201
00:56:17,600 --> 00:56:21,320
But every process is actually
just a data structure

1202
00:56:21,320 --> 00:56:21,940
in the end.

1203
00:56:21,940 --> 00:56:25,160
So this is a representation of
a Linux process, representation

1204
00:56:25,160 --> 00:56:28,120
of some part of what might
be on the GPU at the bottom.

1205
00:56:28,120 --> 00:56:29,833
This is, in the end, just data.

1206
00:56:29,833 --> 00:56:32,000
There's some exceptions
with things like TCP sockets

1207
00:56:32,000 --> 00:56:35,260
that require some surgery, but
fundamentally, this is data.

1208
00:56:35,260 --> 00:56:35,880
You create it.

1209
00:56:35,880 --> 00:56:37,882
If you how to
create it, store it,

1210
00:56:37,882 --> 00:56:39,840
and then you can restore
it from storage faster

1211
00:56:39,840 --> 00:56:41,680
than you can recreate it.

1212
00:56:41,680 --> 00:56:44,200
So you have checkpoint
restore technologies,

1213
00:56:44,200 --> 00:56:46,620
like CRIU and CUDA
checkpoint to use here.

1214
00:56:46,620 --> 00:56:49,000
NVIDIA also released
a blog post yesterday

1215
00:56:49,000 --> 00:56:52,560
about their system for
this GPU memory service

1216
00:56:52,560 --> 00:56:56,100
and others as part of
upcoming Dynamo releases,

1217
00:56:56,100 --> 00:57:00,200
So, check out this blog post
and that one for details.

1218
00:57:00,200 --> 00:57:03,600
So this allows you to operate
a much more efficient inference

1219
00:57:03,600 --> 00:57:05,040
deployment.

1220
00:57:05,040 --> 00:57:05,740
All right.

1221
00:57:05,740 --> 00:57:08,040
So we've got the thing deployed.

1222
00:57:08,040 --> 00:57:10,320
And now, how do we
know if it's broken,

1223
00:57:10,320 --> 00:57:12,800
and how do we fix it when it is?

1224
00:57:12,800 --> 00:57:15,280
So classes of bugs that
you're going to see--

1225
00:57:15,280 --> 00:57:18,400
you've got application level
bugs, model quality bugs,

1226
00:57:18,400 --> 00:57:23,020
and performance bugs,
or failures to hit, SLO.

1227
00:57:23,020 --> 00:57:25,900
So application-level bugs, these
are a shared responsibility

1228
00:57:25,900 --> 00:57:29,040
between you and the application
developers and stakeholders.

1229
00:57:29,040 --> 00:57:31,580
So these are very tricky.

1230
00:57:31,580 --> 00:57:34,122
And as somebody who's mostly
working on the infra side,

1231
00:57:34,122 --> 00:57:35,580
I get to avoid
thinking about them.

1232
00:57:35,580 --> 00:57:38,960
For my own applications, The,
things that I run myself,

1233
00:57:38,960 --> 00:57:40,097
they are-- yeah--

1234
00:57:40,097 --> 00:57:41,180
some of the trickier ones.

1235
00:57:41,180 --> 00:57:43,820
They cross machine
learning and correctness

1236
00:57:43,820 --> 00:57:47,660
stuff, user experience things.

1237
00:57:47,660 --> 00:57:48,763
Yeah.

1238
00:57:48,763 --> 00:57:51,180
So the things that you have a
little bit more control over

1239
00:57:51,180 --> 00:57:53,260
or responsibility
for as an inference

1240
00:57:53,260 --> 00:57:55,940
engineer-- model quality bugs.

1241
00:57:55,940 --> 00:57:58,560
The model looks good at the
point that you deploy it,

1242
00:57:58,560 --> 00:58:01,440
and then it starts behaving
badly in production.

1243
00:58:01,440 --> 00:58:03,300
That could just be
because the things

1244
00:58:03,300 --> 00:58:04,940
are different in production.

1245
00:58:04,940 --> 00:58:07,420
So what academics
would call train test

1246
00:58:07,420 --> 00:58:10,540
skew or train serve skew,
but maybe the most common one

1247
00:58:10,540 --> 00:58:13,800
in my experience
is tokenizer bugs.

1248
00:58:13,800 --> 00:58:17,240
Tokens, as I think Albert
pointed out in his talk-- tokens

1249
00:58:17,240 --> 00:58:17,900
are cursed.

1250
00:58:17,900 --> 00:58:18,980
Tokens are bad.

1251
00:58:18,980 --> 00:58:22,880
We wish we didn't have them, but
we have them at least for now.

1252
00:58:22,880 --> 00:58:24,740
And tokenizers
have, if anything,

1253
00:58:24,740 --> 00:58:27,580
gotten-- tokenizers
and chat templates.

1254
00:58:27,580 --> 00:58:31,400
I'm merging these two together,
but you take a string.

1255
00:58:31,400 --> 00:58:34,320
You augment the user input
string or the harnesses input

1256
00:58:34,320 --> 00:58:37,380
string with special
strings or special tokens.

1257
00:58:37,380 --> 00:58:40,040
Those things have only increased
in complexity over time.

1258
00:58:40,040 --> 00:58:42,220
When open source models
are first released,

1259
00:58:42,220 --> 00:58:46,560
these are almost
assuredly buggy.

1260
00:58:46,560 --> 00:58:49,670
And then these are slowly
beaten down over time.

1261
00:58:49,670 --> 00:58:51,920
So that's actually-- if
you're looking for open source

1262
00:58:51,920 --> 00:58:54,240
like contributions, if
you can run the models

1263
00:58:54,240 --> 00:58:57,620
and discover these
tokenizer bugs,

1264
00:58:57,620 --> 00:59:00,200
this is something you can
both report and ideally fix

1265
00:59:00,200 --> 00:59:02,500
in the engines or in
the model definitions.

1266
00:59:02,500 --> 00:59:04,120
It's valuable open
source contribution

1267
00:59:04,120 --> 00:59:05,380
work with many eyes.

1268
00:59:05,380 --> 00:59:08,740
All bugs are shallow,
even tokenizer bugs.

1269
00:59:08,740 --> 00:59:09,240
Yeah.

1270
00:59:09,240 --> 00:59:10,940
So performance bugs, you'll see.

1271
00:59:10,940 --> 00:59:13,020
Yeah, regressions over
time, and then surprising

1272
00:59:13,020 --> 00:59:15,660
cross replica
differences, especially

1273
00:59:15,660 --> 00:59:19,260
in a heterogeneous
computing environment.

1274
00:59:19,260 --> 00:59:22,780
I use the term observability
in describing this-- the way

1275
00:59:22,780 --> 00:59:24,200
to think about debugging.

1276
00:59:24,200 --> 00:59:26,900
Observability
fundamentally means can you

1277
00:59:26,900 --> 00:59:29,140
debug just from the logs.

1278
00:59:29,140 --> 00:59:31,860
Charity majors describes this
as saying that observability

1279
00:59:31,860 --> 00:59:35,080
is dual to steerability
or controllability,

1280
00:59:35,080 --> 00:59:38,820
and this comes from linear
systems analysis for the EE

1281
00:59:38,820 --> 00:59:40,700
people in the room.

1282
00:59:40,700 --> 00:59:43,860
So the idea is,
can you just look

1283
00:59:43,860 --> 00:59:45,720
at what you've logged
about the application,

1284
00:59:45,720 --> 00:59:49,700
figure out what was wrong with
it, fix the code and ship?

1285
00:59:49,700 --> 00:59:51,605
Obviously you would also
run tests and stuff,

1286
00:59:51,605 --> 00:59:52,480
but this is a vision.

1287
00:59:52,480 --> 00:59:55,660
It's a dream-- without having
to come up with a minimal repro

1288
00:59:55,660 --> 00:59:56,320
or whatever.

1289
00:59:56,320 --> 00:59:58,900
Some other way to
take a complex--

1290
00:59:58,900 --> 01:00:02,980
likely distributed
system and debug it.

1291
01:00:02,980 --> 01:00:05,700
So at the application
level, this generally

1292
01:00:05,700 --> 01:00:07,700
has to be bespoke.

1293
01:00:07,700 --> 01:00:09,860
You can roll your own
with OpenTelemetry based

1294
01:00:09,860 --> 01:00:11,580
tooling in Datadog or whatever.

1295
01:00:11,580 --> 01:00:13,520
The same things
you use in general

1296
01:00:13,520 --> 01:00:16,613
often work pretty well here,
but at the application level,

1297
01:00:16,613 --> 01:00:17,780
things are a little weirder.

1298
01:00:17,780 --> 01:00:18,340
They're fuzzier.

1299
01:00:18,340 --> 01:00:19,798
They're more
machine-learning like,

1300
01:00:19,798 --> 01:00:22,540
and there are specialized tools
for this, like Lang, Smith

1301
01:00:22,540 --> 01:00:23,582
and Braintrust.

1302
01:00:23,582 --> 01:00:25,540
I don't have a ton of
experience at this level,

1303
01:00:25,540 --> 01:00:27,373
but I've heard good
things about using those

1304
01:00:27,373 --> 01:00:30,680
and about rolling their
own, so dealer's choice.

1305
01:00:30,680 --> 01:00:32,560
For model quality,
obviously, you

1306
01:00:32,560 --> 01:00:35,480
want to make sure you've
measured model quality ahead

1307
01:00:35,480 --> 01:00:37,960
of time, so you have a
baseline to compare to.

1308
01:00:37,960 --> 01:00:40,800
So running evaluations
before deployment, but then

1309
01:00:40,800 --> 01:00:44,480
yeah, just aggressive logging
of traces and user feedback.

1310
01:00:44,480 --> 01:00:48,760
For tokenizers, I am
begging you log token IDs

1311
01:00:48,760 --> 01:00:50,060
as part of your logging.

1312
01:00:50,060 --> 01:00:52,400
Don't just log the strings.

1313
01:00:52,400 --> 01:00:56,160
You will have trouble recreating
those token IDs later.

1314
01:00:56,160 --> 01:01:01,080
And this is bugs that are very
subtle expressed in Unicode

1315
01:01:01,080 --> 01:01:03,760
are very obvious--
expressed in token IDs.

1316
01:01:03,760 --> 01:01:06,320
Where did that number come from?

1317
01:01:06,320 --> 01:01:07,680
So log that.

1318
01:01:07,680 --> 01:01:10,800
Performance-- again, log
more metrics than you need.

1319
01:01:10,800 --> 01:01:14,500
The more stuff you
put, the more likely

1320
01:01:14,500 --> 01:01:17,180
it is that you have logged
the one smoking gun that

1321
01:01:17,180 --> 01:01:19,180
points to the actual problem.

1322
01:01:19,180 --> 01:01:22,300
So I think of it as log the
initial object in all things

1323
01:01:22,300 --> 01:01:27,000
you might want to log, and then
you'll filter down from there.

1324
01:01:27,000 --> 01:01:27,500
Yeah.

1325
01:01:27,500 --> 01:01:31,100
On evals-- just
a side note here,

1326
01:01:31,100 --> 01:01:32,560
more at the application level.

1327
01:01:32,560 --> 01:01:34,460
This is how you
build observability

1328
01:01:34,460 --> 01:01:36,260
for all maps at the high-level.

1329
01:01:36,260 --> 01:01:41,680
Evals in many ways they are like
tests in that they're thankless.

1330
01:01:41,680 --> 01:01:42,920
They're annoying to write.

1331
01:01:42,920 --> 01:01:44,000
They cramp your style.

1332
01:01:44,000 --> 01:01:46,220
They're difficult and
time consuming to produce.

1333
01:01:46,220 --> 01:01:48,420
It's low status
labor, but they're

1334
01:01:48,420 --> 01:01:51,505
critical to developing a
successful application.

1335
01:01:51,505 --> 01:01:53,380
And so you can start
with something as simple

1336
01:01:53,380 --> 01:01:55,940
as a Jupyter Notebook
or an Excel spreadsheet

1337
01:01:55,940 --> 01:01:58,180
with some prompts
or whatever in it.

1338
01:01:58,180 --> 01:02:00,820
And that will already
deliver some value

1339
01:02:00,820 --> 01:02:02,860
before you build
the whole system.

1340
01:02:02,860 --> 01:02:05,460
And it is useful for debugging
deployments and knowing

1341
01:02:05,460 --> 01:02:08,160
what kind of performance
you should expect,

1342
01:02:08,160 --> 01:02:11,920
but it's also something you--
if you make the model agnostic,

1343
01:02:11,920 --> 01:02:15,400
you can use them to derive those
more application and product

1344
01:02:15,400 --> 01:02:17,060
level decisions.

1345
01:02:17,060 --> 01:02:18,580
Which model should we use?

1346
01:02:18,580 --> 01:02:22,640
Or what should we run it on?

1347
01:02:22,640 --> 01:02:25,520
How much can we
quantize this model?

1348
01:02:25,520 --> 01:02:27,560
Fundamentally, models
and deployments

1349
01:02:27,560 --> 01:02:29,440
have turned out to
be pretty temporary.

1350
01:02:29,440 --> 01:02:30,800
They're swapped in and out.

1351
01:02:30,800 --> 01:02:32,160
Evals are forever.

1352
01:02:32,160 --> 01:02:34,400
In some sense, they always
last longer than models

1353
01:02:34,400 --> 01:02:36,775
because they have to at least
last long enough to compare

1354
01:02:36,775 --> 01:02:38,805
one model to another.

1355
01:02:38,805 --> 01:02:40,680
So yeah, use them for--
and then you can also

1356
01:02:40,680 --> 01:02:42,440
use them for QA with all
the other things you're

1357
01:02:42,440 --> 01:02:44,940
going to be doing to improve
your inference deployment,

1358
01:02:44,940 --> 01:02:47,220
whether that's fine
tuning, distillation,

1359
01:02:47,220 --> 01:02:51,440
benchmarking, like unhinged
lossy optimizations,

1360
01:02:51,440 --> 01:02:53,420
or speculative models.

1361
01:02:53,420 --> 01:02:55,680
More on that in a second.

1362
01:02:55,680 --> 01:02:57,600
Quick set of metrics to log--

1363
01:02:57,600 --> 01:02:58,500
rough and ready.

1364
01:02:58,500 --> 01:03:00,750
The things we already
mentioned-- time to first token,

1365
01:03:00,750 --> 01:03:02,960
time per output token,
or inter token latency,

1366
01:03:02,960 --> 01:03:06,880
time to last token, request per
second, or queries per second.

1367
01:03:06,880 --> 01:03:10,400
So requests per second is
more at the user level.

1368
01:03:10,400 --> 01:03:12,800
Queries per second is more
at the language model level.

1369
01:03:12,800 --> 01:03:15,580
Anyway, you definitely define
those two terms separately

1370
01:03:15,580 --> 01:03:18,742
and think at the level of the
user or outside of the system

1371
01:03:18,742 --> 01:03:20,700
and then within the system
when you have things

1372
01:03:20,700 --> 01:03:23,020
like tool calls returning.

1373
01:03:23,020 --> 01:03:26,540
Queuing-- in general,
many of these systems

1374
01:03:26,540 --> 01:03:31,820
from the GPU and its SMs, all
the way up to your Kubernetes,

1375
01:03:31,820 --> 01:03:34,080
like your ingress or whatever.

1376
01:03:34,080 --> 01:03:37,020
There will be queues, and they
will be a major contributor

1377
01:03:37,020 --> 01:03:38,113
to your tail latencies.

1378
01:03:38,113 --> 01:03:40,780
So when you want to debug while
your time to first token or time

1379
01:03:40,780 --> 01:03:42,320
to last token is going way up.

1380
01:03:42,320 --> 01:03:45,300
It's probably there's
a queue somewhere.

1381
01:03:45,300 --> 01:03:49,160
Then you want to these split in
the workload prefill and decode.

1382
01:03:49,160 --> 01:03:51,940
You want to when those things
are running and what volume--

1383
01:03:51,940 --> 01:03:52,900
cached, prefill.

1384
01:03:52,900 --> 01:03:58,140
How often are we actually seeing
a prefix that we'd seen before?

1385
01:03:58,140 --> 01:04:00,420
If you really want
to get more advanced,

1386
01:04:00,420 --> 01:04:03,140
log all your prefixes
forever, and then offline

1387
01:04:03,140 --> 01:04:05,240
run-- what would be
the optimal prefix

1388
01:04:05,240 --> 01:04:08,760
hit rate if I had an
infinitely sized cache

1389
01:04:08,760 --> 01:04:13,585
and perfect detection to
compare those numbers to.

1390
01:04:13,585 --> 01:04:15,460
It's nice to have a goal
you can never reach.

1391
01:04:15,460 --> 01:04:18,360
It gives you something to do
for the rest of your life.

1392
01:04:18,360 --> 01:04:21,407
Then you want these metrics
at a per replica level,

1393
01:04:21,407 --> 01:04:23,240
and then you want them
at an aggregate level

1394
01:04:23,240 --> 01:04:25,680
because they're
generally sensible at--

1395
01:04:25,680 --> 01:04:28,900
aggregates give you a sense
for the overall distribution,

1396
01:04:28,900 --> 01:04:30,522
and they allow you
to compare replicas

1397
01:04:30,522 --> 01:04:32,480
to the current state of
things, not just things

1398
01:04:32,480 --> 01:04:34,040
you've measured offline.

1399
01:04:34,040 --> 01:04:37,400
And then you want to measure
these in median and in tail

1400
01:04:37,400 --> 01:04:38,480
and as averages.

1401
01:04:38,480 --> 01:04:41,680
Averages help you compute things
like throughputs and right

1402
01:04:41,680 --> 01:04:43,240
sizing.

1403
01:04:43,240 --> 01:04:45,760
And the meat and the
quantiles give you

1404
01:04:45,760 --> 01:04:47,960
a sense for user experience.

1405
01:04:47,960 --> 01:04:51,440
And as I showed with the
little demo of the token timing

1406
01:04:51,440 --> 01:04:55,760
simulator, those tail
latencies, despite them

1407
01:04:55,760 --> 01:04:58,620
looking like small
numbers, like 5%,

1408
01:04:58,620 --> 01:05:00,760
they're really most of the time.

1409
01:05:00,760 --> 01:05:03,480
And then lastly on the
hardware level-- temperature,

1410
01:05:03,480 --> 01:05:05,580
power-- these things matter.

1411
01:05:05,580 --> 01:05:07,240
Not every deployment
gets these right.

1412
01:05:07,240 --> 01:05:08,680
There are problems.

1413
01:05:08,680 --> 01:05:10,500
There's bad cooling.

1414
01:05:10,500 --> 01:05:11,640
You want to watch this.

1415
01:05:11,640 --> 01:05:15,740
And then utilization of things,
like the memory capacity

1416
01:05:15,740 --> 01:05:18,220
and the kernels.

1417
01:05:18,220 --> 01:05:21,400
Sorry-- the utilization of CUDA
streams, kernel utilization.

1418
01:05:21,400 --> 01:05:24,267
How often is work
running on the GPU?

1419
01:05:24,267 --> 01:05:26,600
These are the things that you
can measure really easily,

1420
01:05:26,600 --> 01:05:29,693
asynchronously with
low or no overhead.

1421
01:05:29,693 --> 01:05:31,860
The other things you might
want to measure generally

1422
01:05:31,860 --> 01:05:34,580
have additional overhead,
at least for now.

1423
01:05:34,580 --> 01:05:37,340
And so you can't get the lowest
level performance counters

1424
01:05:37,340 --> 01:05:38,800
without taking a
performance hit.

1425
01:05:38,800 --> 01:05:42,300
That's generally unacceptable.

1426
01:05:42,300 --> 01:05:45,980
So this is a little dashboard
for this on an internal alpha

1427
01:05:45,980 --> 01:05:48,860
that we've built at modal
for tracking these things.

1428
01:05:48,860 --> 01:05:53,320
This is a recent fun deployment
I had of a multi-modal model.

1429
01:05:53,320 --> 01:05:58,040
You can see there's lots of
spikes in the P95 and P99,

1430
01:05:58,040 --> 01:06:00,800
even though the
P50s look very fine.

1431
01:06:00,800 --> 01:06:04,440
And then you can see a couple of
things that jump out from this.

1432
01:06:04,440 --> 01:06:07,920
It's the inter token
latencies are correlated

1433
01:06:07,920 --> 01:06:11,720
in time with queuing, so that
suggests that the latencies that

1434
01:06:11,720 --> 01:06:14,680
are very long are actually
a request it was generating

1435
01:06:14,680 --> 01:06:16,313
tokens, and then
it got kicked out

1436
01:06:16,313 --> 01:06:17,980
so something else
could generate tokens,

1437
01:06:17,980 --> 01:06:19,400
and it got cycled back in.

1438
01:06:19,400 --> 01:06:21,200
This is a behavior
inference engines will

1439
01:06:21,200 --> 01:06:24,200
do to try to achieve fair
distribution of token throughput

1440
01:06:24,200 --> 01:06:26,000
to requests.

1441
01:06:26,000 --> 01:06:27,600
And that's bad.

1442
01:06:27,600 --> 01:06:30,880
That maybe means I have
too many requests going

1443
01:06:30,880 --> 01:06:34,237
to the individual replicas
here, and I need to lower that.

1444
01:06:34,237 --> 01:06:36,320
Or it could be that there's
something slowing down

1445
01:06:36,320 --> 01:06:37,440
in the replicas.

1446
01:06:37,440 --> 01:06:39,680
I didn't fully debug this
because the performance

1447
01:06:39,680 --> 01:06:44,560
was acceptable for the
domain, which was an art show.

1448
01:06:44,560 --> 01:06:46,960
Yeah.

1449
01:06:46,960 --> 01:06:48,040
All right.

1450
01:06:48,040 --> 01:06:51,660
So speaking of optimizing
performance, how do you do this?

1451
01:06:51,660 --> 01:06:54,300
Not just like looking
at those charts there,

1452
01:06:54,300 --> 01:06:57,520
but then how do you fix the
problems that you observe?

1453
01:06:57,520 --> 01:06:59,880
So with performance
optimization, I think,

1454
01:06:59,880 --> 01:07:04,820
yeah, the same lessons that
are elsewhere really apply.

1455
01:07:04,820 --> 01:07:07,140
There are big things
that you can work on,

1456
01:07:07,140 --> 01:07:09,220
and the biggest things
are speculative decoding

1457
01:07:09,220 --> 01:07:10,480
and quantization.

1458
01:07:10,480 --> 01:07:13,160
These are generally--
their low hanging fruit,

1459
01:07:13,160 --> 01:07:15,283
but they often are
application specific.

1460
01:07:15,283 --> 01:07:16,700
And that means
that you can't just

1461
01:07:16,700 --> 01:07:20,220
stand on the shoulders of
giants like you can elsewhere.

1462
01:07:20,220 --> 01:07:24,140
Then there's the grind
of medium benefit tasks

1463
01:07:24,140 --> 01:07:27,620
that involve engineering on
the host rather than the GPU,

1464
01:07:27,620 --> 01:07:29,220
and then only after
that should you

1465
01:07:29,220 --> 01:07:31,700
start thinking about
doing GPU side work

1466
01:07:31,700 --> 01:07:34,700
and doing things like kernel
optimization, where you'll

1467
01:07:34,700 --> 01:07:37,780
get a few percentage points
speedups, which really matters

1468
01:07:37,780 --> 01:07:39,980
at very large scale but
only once you've gotten

1469
01:07:39,980 --> 01:07:42,180
the big things out of the way.

1470
01:07:42,180 --> 01:07:45,940
So let's talk about these
in a little bit more detail.

1471
01:07:45,940 --> 01:07:49,560
So apologies-- this was a light
mode diagram that I copy pasted.

1472
01:07:49,560 --> 01:07:52,980
So speculative decoding--
tries to solve this problem

1473
01:07:52,980 --> 01:07:57,000
where decode has too
few operations per byte.

1474
01:07:57,000 --> 01:08:01,860
And the solution-- and if you're
more of an algorithms person,

1475
01:08:01,860 --> 01:08:05,880
it's that you have one or a
small number of query vectors

1476
01:08:05,880 --> 01:08:08,800
in your attention.

1477
01:08:08,800 --> 01:08:14,720
And yeah, and one token
in your MLPs or MOE.

1478
01:08:14,720 --> 01:08:18,060
And so the solution
then is, all right--

1479
01:08:18,060 --> 01:08:19,460
so we aren't using
all the flops,

1480
01:08:19,460 --> 01:08:22,359
but we are using all
the memory bandwidth.

1481
01:08:22,359 --> 01:08:26,040
What if we gave those extra
flops something to do,

1482
01:08:26,040 --> 01:08:30,439
even if it wasn't
always useful work?

1483
01:08:30,439 --> 01:08:32,520
So what if we guessed
what the next things

1484
01:08:32,520 --> 01:08:36,340
to come after Modal is a
serverless computing company?

1485
01:08:36,340 --> 01:08:37,040
We guess that.

1486
01:08:37,040 --> 01:08:40,600
We put it in, and we run on
those speculated or drafted

1487
01:08:40,600 --> 01:08:41,760
tokens.

1488
01:08:41,760 --> 01:08:44,840
And we run the full
model, the model

1489
01:08:44,840 --> 01:08:46,819
we want to get
tokens from on that.

1490
01:08:46,819 --> 01:08:49,200
And then we run a
Metropolis-Hastings style

1491
01:08:49,200 --> 01:08:51,000
rejection sampling
algorithm that

1492
01:08:51,000 --> 01:08:54,120
guarantees the tokens
that we get out

1493
01:08:54,120 --> 01:08:57,100
have the exact same
probability distribution

1494
01:08:57,100 --> 01:08:58,859
as the tokens that went in.

1495
01:08:58,859 --> 01:09:01,420
For greedy decoding, that
just means take all the tokens

1496
01:09:01,420 --> 01:09:03,920
that agree in a prefix.

1497
01:09:03,920 --> 01:09:05,819
So if you have
temperature equals 0,

1498
01:09:05,819 --> 01:09:09,240
it's just take
serverless computing.

1499
01:09:09,240 --> 01:09:12,479
And then the first
token that's different,

1500
01:09:12,479 --> 01:09:15,939
use one from the target model.

1501
01:09:15,939 --> 01:09:18,300
So when you are at--

1502
01:09:18,300 --> 01:09:20,180
when you are memory
bound, which is generally

1503
01:09:20,180 --> 01:09:22,140
going to start to
show up when you have

1504
01:09:22,140 --> 01:09:27,979
very tight latency budgets and
smaller models on big GPUs,

1505
01:09:27,979 --> 01:09:33,060
this becomes like an absolute
no brainer to implement.

1506
01:09:33,060 --> 01:09:37,020
And the reason why I say
speculation is all you need

1507
01:09:37,020 --> 01:09:42,220
is because it gives you these
very large, integral measured

1508
01:09:42,220 --> 01:09:43,380
speed up factors.

1509
01:09:43,380 --> 01:09:47,660
People talk about
speedups of 2x, 4x, 8x.

1510
01:09:47,660 --> 01:09:52,340
And it is also, as
my colleague David

1511
01:09:52,340 --> 01:09:56,680
Wang put it, crazy bitter
lesson pilled, which

1512
01:09:56,680 --> 01:10:02,720
is to say to create those draft
tokens, you need another model.

1513
01:10:02,720 --> 01:10:06,300
And that model
needs to be trained,

1514
01:10:06,300 --> 01:10:09,540
and it will get better with
more data and more compute,

1515
01:10:09,540 --> 01:10:12,160
just like the base models do.

1516
01:10:12,160 --> 01:10:16,320
And so using application
specific data,

1517
01:10:16,320 --> 01:10:20,840
you can increase your speed
up from speculative decoding

1518
01:10:20,840 --> 01:10:23,360
from a factor of 2 to
a factor of 6, which

1519
01:10:23,360 --> 01:10:25,260
is a factor of 3 speed up.

1520
01:10:25,260 --> 01:10:26,300
That's very big.

1521
01:10:26,300 --> 01:10:31,200
It's the difference between does
this application work or not.

1522
01:10:31,200 --> 01:10:33,780
And so we're very big believers.

1523
01:10:33,780 --> 01:10:36,800
This math here, this is the
simplest mathematical model

1524
01:10:36,800 --> 01:10:38,840
of speculative
decoding that gives you

1525
01:10:38,840 --> 01:10:43,185
this exponential speed up from
increased acceptance lengths.

1526
01:10:43,185 --> 01:10:45,060
We do observe things
like this in production.

1527
01:10:45,060 --> 01:10:49,440
Detailed mathematical models
have things diminishing returns

1528
01:10:49,440 --> 01:10:55,500
or whatever, but you do observe
very substantial speed ups

1529
01:10:55,500 --> 01:10:58,460
from custom speculators.

1530
01:10:58,460 --> 01:11:01,060
And this in addition
to the release

1531
01:11:01,060 --> 01:11:06,340
of things like SGLang and BLM
and their excellent engineering

1532
01:11:06,340 --> 01:11:09,637
work on those, the creation of
really good speculative models

1533
01:11:09,637 --> 01:11:11,220
is one of the other
reasons why people

1534
01:11:11,220 --> 01:11:13,500
can run their own
inference now in a way

1535
01:11:13,500 --> 01:11:15,380
that they couldn't
a year or two ago.

1536
01:11:15,380 --> 01:11:17,740
So there are many
approaches to speculation.

1537
01:11:17,740 --> 01:11:21,740
Simple one is n-gram,
a language model

1538
01:11:21,740 --> 01:11:25,460
you might learn about in a
math class or a stats class.

1539
01:11:25,460 --> 01:11:28,800
Just repeat sequences of tokens
that appeared in the prompt.

1540
01:11:28,800 --> 01:11:30,660
If you see n minus
1 tokens, guess

1541
01:11:30,660 --> 01:11:32,780
the next token is the n-th.

1542
01:11:32,780 --> 01:11:37,220
That works OK, but
nowadays, many models

1543
01:11:37,220 --> 01:11:39,920
are released with multi-token
prediction built in,

1544
01:11:39,920 --> 01:11:41,660
so the last heads of
the model don't just

1545
01:11:41,660 --> 01:11:43,000
produce a single token.

1546
01:11:43,000 --> 01:11:44,080
They produce several.

1547
01:11:44,080 --> 01:11:47,980
This has benefits at train time
and at inference time, which

1548
01:11:47,980 --> 01:11:49,000
is really nice.

1549
01:11:49,000 --> 01:11:53,160
Usually these are two groups of
engineers who hate each other.

1550
01:11:53,160 --> 01:11:55,380
But yeah, so this is
becoming almost standard.

1551
01:11:55,380 --> 01:11:58,040
It's generally
released with things.

1552
01:11:58,040 --> 01:12:01,720
Though it's a little
tricky sometimes to train

1553
01:12:01,720 --> 01:12:03,800
multi-token prediction.

1554
01:12:03,800 --> 01:12:07,260
And you can do a little bit
better at inference time.

1555
01:12:07,260 --> 01:12:11,240
What people have
found is that if you

1556
01:12:11,240 --> 01:12:13,600
train your draft model
based off of the hidden

1557
01:12:13,600 --> 01:12:17,140
states of the model, even
the earlier hidden states.

1558
01:12:17,140 --> 01:12:19,040
Interesting phenomenon
in these models

1559
01:12:19,040 --> 01:12:21,960
is like with depth, they
become more specialized to just

1560
01:12:21,960 --> 01:12:23,720
predicting the next token.

1561
01:12:23,720 --> 01:12:27,720
Over time, they're doing
interesting combinations

1562
01:12:27,720 --> 01:12:28,980
of reasoning across tokens.

1563
01:12:28,980 --> 01:12:31,460
They maybe don't know what
the next token is going to be.

1564
01:12:31,460 --> 01:12:34,540
The know the next idea or
the next couple of ideas,

1565
01:12:34,540 --> 01:12:36,700
and that sharpens over
time as you go deeper.

1566
01:12:36,700 --> 01:12:39,680
Look at some of the model
surgery papers for stuff

1567
01:12:39,680 --> 01:12:42,200
about that-- layer skipping.

1568
01:12:42,200 --> 01:12:44,460
So you actually want the
earlier layers in there.

1569
01:12:44,460 --> 01:12:45,860
And that's tricky
to do with MTP,

1570
01:12:45,860 --> 01:12:48,140
but it's easy to do offline.

1571
01:12:48,140 --> 01:12:50,820
Eagle is one of the first
things that did this.

1572
01:12:50,820 --> 01:12:54,620
So you train this thing
alongside the model.

1573
01:12:54,620 --> 01:12:57,220
And then D flash is
the one that we've

1574
01:12:57,220 --> 01:12:59,180
gotten excited about at Modal.

1575
01:12:59,180 --> 01:13:02,340
It's like Eagle, but it's like
a denoising diffusion language

1576
01:13:02,340 --> 01:13:03,620
model.

1577
01:13:03,620 --> 01:13:05,740
And diffusion language
models have generally

1578
01:13:05,740 --> 01:13:08,520
struggled to reach the
highest capabilities,

1579
01:13:08,520 --> 01:13:12,520
but diffusion is generally
a better fit for hardware.

1580
01:13:12,520 --> 01:13:14,800
It has a higher
arithmetic intensity--

1581
01:13:14,800 --> 01:13:16,440
naturally maps better.

1582
01:13:16,440 --> 01:13:19,780
You don't have that
prefilled decode split.

1583
01:13:19,780 --> 01:13:22,320
So I'm really
excited about that.

1584
01:13:22,320 --> 01:13:25,740
And so we're big
in on that and have

1585
01:13:25,740 --> 01:13:29,580
been working on things like
improvements with the authors

1586
01:13:29,580 --> 01:13:34,420
and on getting it well
integrated into SGLang.

1587
01:13:34,420 --> 01:13:35,740
So that's speculation.

1588
01:13:35,740 --> 01:13:39,000
Quantization-- that is your
other biggest lever to pull.

1589
01:13:39,000 --> 01:13:45,480
If you can go from FP8 to FP4,
that's like a 2x speed up.

1590
01:13:45,480 --> 01:13:48,680
And that's a 2x speed up
both for memory bound things

1591
01:13:48,680 --> 01:13:50,480
and for compute bound things.

1592
01:13:50,480 --> 01:13:52,000
So for memory bound
things, you're

1593
01:13:52,000 --> 01:13:54,080
cutting the memory
bandwidth demand in half

1594
01:13:54,080 --> 01:13:55,920
because there's fewer bytes.

1595
01:13:55,920 --> 01:14:00,920
And Tensor Cores and
other floating point math,

1596
01:14:00,920 --> 01:14:03,460
they are quoted in
an ops per second,

1597
01:14:03,460 --> 01:14:08,300
and that ops per second scales
linearly with the bytes.

1598
01:14:08,300 --> 01:14:11,160
So really, there's bytes
of operations per second

1599
01:14:11,160 --> 01:14:13,280
that they actually
have as bandwidth.

1600
01:14:13,280 --> 01:14:16,120
So the same thing
basically applies.

1601
01:14:16,120 --> 01:14:19,360
So this unfortunately requires
full stack coordination

1602
01:14:19,360 --> 01:14:20,920
for a couple of reasons.

1603
01:14:20,920 --> 01:14:24,280
One is that if you want to
operate with FP4 with hardware

1604
01:14:24,280 --> 01:14:29,240
acceleration, you need Blackwell
GPUs in the NVIDIA stack.

1605
01:14:29,240 --> 01:14:32,680
So like this-- and
on the other hand,

1606
01:14:32,680 --> 01:14:35,480
quantization changes
model behavior.

1607
01:14:35,480 --> 01:14:39,280
So you have to think about this
at the application layer, right?

1608
01:14:39,280 --> 01:14:41,560
And so you can't just--

1609
01:14:41,560 --> 01:14:48,060
the evals, or vibe
checks, or testing

1610
01:14:48,060 --> 01:14:51,620
in prod-- that is the handoff
between the application

1611
01:14:51,620 --> 01:14:54,140
layer and the hardware layer.

1612
01:14:54,140 --> 01:14:56,580
You need to make sure that
that's good enough that you can

1613
01:14:56,580 --> 01:14:59,380
confidently ship this change.

1614
01:14:59,380 --> 01:15:04,700
A couple things about the
model behavior changes.

1615
01:15:04,700 --> 01:15:06,520
Generally, it's worse
on longer sequences.

1616
01:15:06,520 --> 01:15:08,020
You have lower precision.

1617
01:15:08,020 --> 01:15:10,320
With longer sequences, you
have more accumulation.

1618
01:15:10,320 --> 01:15:12,620
So there's more
accumulated error.

1619
01:15:12,620 --> 01:15:15,860
And so whether it's
in or out sequences,

1620
01:15:15,860 --> 01:15:17,717
you're going to
struggle more here.

1621
01:15:17,717 --> 01:15:19,300
But that means with
shorter sequences,

1622
01:15:19,300 --> 01:15:21,860
you can do this with
more confidence.

1623
01:15:21,860 --> 01:15:23,262
FP8 is pretty standard.

1624
01:15:23,262 --> 01:15:24,720
It's supported on
hopper and above.

1625
01:15:24,720 --> 01:15:26,400
FP4 is relatively cutting edge.

1626
01:15:26,400 --> 01:15:31,380
This is generally done in
only the MLP or MOE layers,

1627
01:15:31,380 --> 01:15:32,800
and not in the attention layers.

1628
01:15:32,800 --> 01:15:35,340
Attention involves a
lot more accumulation,

1629
01:15:35,340 --> 01:15:37,700
and so it's a
little bit trickier.

1630
01:15:37,700 --> 01:15:42,280
And so yeah, but matmuls are
generally the bottleneck or more

1631
01:15:42,280 --> 01:15:43,460
frequently the bottleneck.

1632
01:15:43,460 --> 01:15:47,280
And so you still see the
roughly linear speed up.

1633
01:15:47,280 --> 01:15:49,840
You might want to do
things like quantize

1634
01:15:49,840 --> 01:15:51,680
or compress the KV cache.

1635
01:15:51,680 --> 01:15:54,100
This is pretty hard.

1636
01:15:54,100 --> 01:15:56,600
It should generally be
done, I think, at train time

1637
01:15:56,600 --> 01:16:00,160
as the DeepSeek V4 model does.

1638
01:16:00,160 --> 01:16:03,720
Just like-- yeah, it is
trickier to get right

1639
01:16:03,720 --> 01:16:06,200
than weight quantization.

1640
01:16:06,200 --> 01:16:09,320
And for all these
things, this is very much

1641
01:16:09,320 --> 01:16:11,260
the current state of affairs.

1642
01:16:11,260 --> 01:16:15,980
And to my knowledge, I might
have missed recent papers.

1643
01:16:15,980 --> 01:16:16,480
OK.

1644
01:16:16,480 --> 01:16:24,120
So closing out here,
yeah, the next tier

1645
01:16:24,120 --> 01:16:27,320
is getting the CPU out
of the way of the GPU.

1646
01:16:27,320 --> 01:16:28,960
Don't block it.

1647
01:16:28,960 --> 01:16:32,200
So you're basically submitting
a bunch of work to the GPU.

1648
01:16:32,200 --> 01:16:35,800
Make sure that there's always
something for it to do.

1649
01:16:35,800 --> 01:16:37,640
Key technique for this
CUDA graph capture

1650
01:16:37,640 --> 01:16:40,600
turns many CUDA kernel
launches into one.

1651
01:16:40,600 --> 01:16:43,020
This is from a profiler showing
many CUDA graphs launched

1652
01:16:43,020 --> 01:16:44,340
at once.

1653
01:16:44,340 --> 01:16:47,980
So make sure to turn that one
on in your inference engine.

1654
01:16:47,980 --> 01:16:51,780
Key tool for looking
for host overhead

1655
01:16:51,780 --> 01:16:58,720
is GPU power draw or temperature
not being as high of a level.

1656
01:16:58,720 --> 01:17:00,700
So these are two
replicas, one of which

1657
01:17:00,700 --> 01:17:02,355
was running slower
than the other.

1658
01:17:02,355 --> 01:17:03,980
And the second replica
was only drawing

1659
01:17:03,980 --> 01:17:06,520
2 kilowatts instead of
the full three kilowatts

1660
01:17:06,520 --> 01:17:09,500
plus it should have
been able to draw.

1661
01:17:09,500 --> 01:17:14,380
And taking a look at that with
a tool like the Torch Profiler

1662
01:17:14,380 --> 01:17:17,460
that I think you saw in previous
lectures, or the Nsight systems

1663
01:17:17,460 --> 01:17:20,460
profiler, both of which are
built on the same CUDA profiling

1664
01:17:20,460 --> 01:17:22,740
tools interface from NVIDIA--

1665
01:17:22,740 --> 01:17:25,140
able to determine
that one of the causes

1666
01:17:25,140 --> 01:17:32,740
was that one of the workers
that was participating

1667
01:17:32,740 --> 01:17:36,100
in a collective was
struggling behind the others.

1668
01:17:36,100 --> 01:17:38,040
I haven't fully
actually debug this one.

1669
01:17:38,040 --> 01:17:41,240
This is a bit of a
live this week problem,

1670
01:17:41,240 --> 01:17:44,720
but suspect NUMA
Affinity problems.

1671
01:17:44,720 --> 01:17:47,440
And so NUMA Affinity
is a very hard thing

1672
01:17:47,440 --> 01:17:52,560
to know about, especially if
you have heterogeneous hardware.

1673
01:17:52,560 --> 01:17:57,120
But temperature or power
draw is very easy and free.

1674
01:17:57,120 --> 01:18:00,233
So watch this guy
and then dive in.

1675
01:18:00,233 --> 01:18:01,900
But you don't have
to go get that fancy.

1676
01:18:01,900 --> 01:18:03,720
You can literally just
use something like py-spy

1677
01:18:03,720 --> 01:18:05,095
to watch what's
going on the host

1678
01:18:05,095 --> 01:18:07,160
and look for bottlenecks
that you can optimize.

1679
01:18:07,160 --> 01:18:11,040
We have a blog post about how
we came up with a very simple,

1680
01:18:11,040 --> 01:18:14,960
like, Python-only solution to
speed up multimodal inference

1681
01:18:14,960 --> 01:18:22,040
in SGLanf by over 10% by just
like caching a pointer basically

1682
01:18:22,040 --> 01:18:27,480
in a Python dict, instead of
recreating it on every request--

1683
01:18:27,480 --> 01:18:30,760
every tensor on every request.

1684
01:18:30,760 --> 01:18:38,740
And that, yeah, no CUDA mode,
no Nsight profiler, just py-spy.

1685
01:18:38,740 --> 01:18:41,820
So only then is it time to
consider GPU optimization

1686
01:18:41,820 --> 01:18:42,600
kernels out there.

1687
01:18:42,600 --> 01:18:43,433
They're pretty good.

1688
01:18:43,433 --> 01:18:45,480
They're often very close
to the speed of light.

1689
01:18:45,480 --> 01:18:48,240
You have lots of people
trying to work on these,

1690
01:18:48,240 --> 01:18:50,847
and they're very
reusable in general.

1691
01:18:50,847 --> 01:18:52,680
So there's lots of good
libraries for these.

1692
01:18:52,680 --> 01:18:54,638
And that means there are
only percentage points

1693
01:18:54,638 --> 01:18:56,200
to gain in most cases.

1694
01:18:56,200 --> 01:18:59,900
So only get to it when
percentage points are

1695
01:18:59,900 --> 01:19:01,200
the thing that you care about.

1696
01:19:01,200 --> 01:19:03,940

1697
01:19:03,940 --> 01:19:05,845
The tool for that--
the primary tool

1698
01:19:05,845 --> 01:19:08,220
for that being Nsight compute,
which can help you measure

1699
01:19:08,220 --> 01:19:10,340
the lowest level hardware
utilization-- actually

1700
01:19:10,340 --> 01:19:13,060
measure things how much of the
arithmetic bandwidth and memory

1701
01:19:13,060 --> 01:19:15,820
bandwidth am I using and map
things about your performance

1702
01:19:15,820 --> 01:19:19,980
directly onto, say, the
assembler to look for pipeline

1703
01:19:19,980 --> 01:19:23,980
stalls or scoreboard stalls.

1704
01:19:23,980 --> 01:19:25,100
Yeah.

1705
01:19:25,100 --> 01:19:29,140
And just to point again
at before you go this--

1706
01:19:29,140 --> 01:19:32,900
before you pull the fancy
tools out, the way [INAUDIBLE]

1707
01:19:32,900 --> 01:19:35,960
writes fast kernels
is with a whiteboard.

1708
01:19:35,960 --> 01:19:38,262
So think about what
are the bottlenecks.

1709
01:19:38,262 --> 01:19:40,220
What are the most important
things to optimize?

1710
01:19:40,220 --> 01:19:42,720
What speed are they able
to achieve and reason

1711
01:19:42,720 --> 01:19:46,840
from there before pulling
out the empirical techniques.

1712
01:19:46,840 --> 01:19:49,880
So check out their blog
on that for details.

1713
01:19:49,880 --> 01:19:50,380
OK.

1714
01:19:50,380 --> 01:19:52,240
So I don't have much time.

1715
01:19:52,240 --> 01:19:54,140
And we were-- yeah.

1716
01:19:54,140 --> 01:19:56,800
I will stick around
for extra questions.

1717
01:19:56,800 --> 01:20:00,840
So I'll just say what's next--

1718
01:20:00,840 --> 01:20:02,820
lossy weird optimizations.

1719
01:20:02,820 --> 01:20:06,200
Skip layers, quantize
things, prune,

1720
01:20:06,200 --> 01:20:10,800
lossy speculative decoding, mega
kernels for speculative coding,

1721
01:20:10,800 --> 01:20:13,680
and draft models are
ready for specific layers

1722
01:20:13,680 --> 01:20:16,400
in the future for whole models.

1723
01:20:16,400 --> 01:20:19,020
I said NVIDIA GPUs
are your only option,

1724
01:20:19,020 --> 01:20:21,920
but that's very much
only true right now.

1725
01:20:21,920 --> 01:20:25,480
NVIDIA is already going to
be releasing a hybrid rack

1726
01:20:25,480 --> 01:20:29,840
in the next generation that
includes LPUs from Grok.

1727
01:20:29,840 --> 01:20:32,680
That actually to assert--
it doesn't actually

1728
01:20:32,680 --> 01:20:34,880
map onto the prefilled
decode split just yet,

1729
01:20:34,880 --> 01:20:38,278
but I think that is basically
an artifact of history.

1730
01:20:38,278 --> 01:20:39,820
And in the future,
prefill and decode

